window.LESSONS=[{"id":"lecture-01","lecture":1,"title":"Course introduction","short":"Course introduction","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"Database systems: motivation, definitions, data abstraction and the scope of the Edinburgh course.","goals":["Motivation for database systems","Database, DBMS and file-based storage","Abstraction, data models and course scope"],"sections":[{"id":"l1-1","title":"Motivation for database systems","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l1-1\"></span><p>Database systems store and process the data used by applications. The course studies the principles that allow this processing to remain efficient as data volume and the number of users grow. These principles include data independence, declarative queries, concurrency and reliable storage.</p><p>The introductory slides motivate this subject through application data, scientific data and the difference between data growth and processing capacity. Their numerical charts describe the sources and dates shown in the slides; they are background context, rather than parameters used by the later algorithms.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–9</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-2","title":"Database, DBMS and file-based storage","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l1-2\"></span><p>A <strong>database</strong> is an organised collection of related data representing a domain. A <strong>database management system (DBMS)</strong> is the software that stores, manages and provides access to that collection. The course mainly concerns relational DBMSs.</p><p>The slides use a university database with Instructor and Course relations. Storing these relations in CSV files requires application code to parse records, search for values and coordinate changes. Repeated facts create redundancy; application code becomes dependent on the file format.</p><p>A DBMS centralises query execution, integrity constraints, atomic updates, concurrent access, permissions and programmatic access. Atomicity prevents a failed operation from leaving only part of a required update. Concurrency control coordinates operations from different users. These are separate system responsibilities.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 10–17</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-3","title":"Abstraction, data models and course scope","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l1-3\"></span><p>The <strong>physical level</strong> describes the representation of records in storage. The <strong>logical level</strong> describes the data and its structure. The <strong>view level</strong> exposes selected information to an application or user. Data independence limits the effect of changes at lower levels on higher levels.</p><p>A data model supplies the concepts used to describe data. A schema describes a particular database using that model; an instance is the current data satisfying the schema. Relational, key-value, document and graph models provide different concepts.</p><p>The technical sequence covers disk-oriented storage, indexes, query evaluation and optimisation, transactions, and distributed and parallel databases. Query theory supplies precise definitions and complexity results. The assumed background includes introductory databases, algorithm analysis and programming.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 18–24</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-4","title":"Edinburgh course organisation and references","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l1-4\"></span><p>The remaining introductory slides describe the Edinburgh Spring 2026 teaching team, lectures, revision classes, assessment, textbooks and communication channels. The lecture sequence has three blocks: database/query-language revision, DBMS internals and query-evaluation theory.</p><p>Ramakrishnan and Gehrke is the main systems reference; the linked database-theory text supports the theory block. The source also states Edinburgh-specific assignment and examination rules. Consult the source for those historical arrangements; they do not establish deadlines or assessment requirements for another university.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 25–36</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Changing a record’s byte position primarily belongs to which level?","options":["View","Logical","Physical"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A byte position is a physical representation detail; the schema describes the meaning and structure of the data."},"source":{"summary":"Course Introduction: motivation, database/DBMS, flat-file weaknesses, abstraction, data models; course organisation, assessment and academic integrity.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pp. 10–19 · databases, DBMSs, and abstraction; no printed slide numbers"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–35 · roadmap and information for the Spring 2026 course"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture01-intro-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–36","pdfPageCount":36,"topics":["Motivation","Flat files","DBMS","Abstraction","Data independence","Data models/schema","Course organisation","Assessment","Academic integrity"],"limitations":["Intro has no printed slide numbers; citations use physical PDF page numbers.","Administrative dates apply to Spring 2026 only."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"Course Introduction","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #1: U TILITY"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #2: C ENTRALITY"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"How much data is"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S CALE OF S CIEN TIFIC D ATA"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #2: C ENTRALITY"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY ? R EASON #3: T HE C ORE OF C OMPUTING"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT IS A D ATABASE ?"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT IS A DBMS?"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HY USING A DBMS?"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE S TRAWMAN"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE S TRAWMAN"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"F LAT F ILE : D RAWBACKS (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L EVELS OF A BSTRACTIONS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"D ATA M ODELS"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"R ANKING OF DBMS T ECHNOLOGIES 2026"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT I S T HIS C OURSE A BOUT ?"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HAT I S T HIS C OURSE A BOUT ?"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L EARNING O UTCOMES"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"C OURSE P REREQUISITES"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"E SSENTIAL Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"W HO I S R UNNING T HIS C OURSE ?"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"H OW W ILL T HIS C OURSE W ORK ?"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"L ECTURE O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"A SSESSMENT S TRUCTURE"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"P ROGRAMMING A SSIGNMENT"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"T EXTBOOKS"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"R ECENT C OURSE C HANGES"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S TUDENT F EEDBACK FROM P REVIOUS Y EARS"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"P LAGIARISM P OLICY"},{"pdfPage":35,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"S TAYING IN T OUCH"},{"pdfPage":36,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"A CKNOWLEDGEMENT"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":null},{"id":"lecture-02","lecture":2,"title":"SQL","short":"SQL","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"Relational terminology, single-table queries, aggregation, joins and nested queries.","goals":["Relational terminology and single-table queries","Aggregation, GROUP BY and HAVING","Queries over multiple tables and joins"],"sections":[{"id":"l2-1","title":"Relational terminology and single-table queries","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l2-1\"></span><p>SQL is declarative: a query specifies the required result while the DBMS chooses an execution algorithm. DDL defines schemas, DML queries and modifies data, and DCL controls access. A relation has a schema of named, typed attributes and an instance of tuples. SQL normally uses <strong>bag semantics</strong>, allowing duplicate rows.</p><p>The source database contains Student(sid, name, dept, age), Enrolled(sid, cid, grade) and Course(cid, name, year). <code>SELECT * FROM Student WHERE age = 18</code> keeps the students satisfying the predicate. SELECT chooses output expressions; DISTINCT removes duplicate output rows.</p><p>ORDER BY sorts by one or more expressions, using ASC or DESC for each. LIMIT restricts the number of output rows; OFFSET skips rows first. Without ORDER BY, the selected subset has no guaranteed order.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l2-2","title":"Aggregation, GROUP BY and HAVING","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l2-2\"></span><p>Aggregate functions summarise a collection of rows: COUNT counts, SUM sums, AVG averages, and MIN/MAX find extrema. GROUP BY partitions rows by the values of the grouping attributes and produces one aggregate result per group. In the standard grouped form taught here, a selected expression must be a grouping expression or an aggregate.</p><p>WHERE filters input rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after aggregate values have been computed. The slide query <code>SELECT dept, AVG(age) FROM Student GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(age) &gt; 21</code> returns only departments whose average age exceeds 21.</p><p>The logical evaluation sequence is FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY/aggregation → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY → LIMIT. It defines the query result; the optimiser may use another physical order when equivalence is preserved.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–18</a></p>"},{"id":"l2-3","title":"Queries over multiple tables and joins","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l2-3\"></span><p>A multi-table FROM clause conceptually forms a Cartesian product; the join predicate restricts it to matching pairs. The source query joins Student and Enrolled on sid and filters <code>E.cid = 'INF-11199'</code> to obtain names and grades. The DBMS can implement this without materialising the full product.</p><p>INNER JOIN returns matching pairs. NATURAL JOIN equates all attributes with matching names and retains one copy of each shared attribute, so its meaning depends on the schemas. Explicit JOIN ... ON makes the intended matching condition visible.</p><p>LEFT OUTER JOIN also preserves unmatched left rows, adding NULL values for the right attributes. RIGHT OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched right rows; FULL OUTER JOIN preserves both. A left row with several matching right rows still produces several result rows.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 19–25</a></p>"},{"id":"l2-4","title":"Nested queries and set comparisons","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l2-4\"></span><p>A nested query uses the result of another query. IN tests membership; EXISTS tests whether a subquery returns any row. A correlated subquery refers to attributes of an outer query. The source expresses enrolment in INF-11199 using either IN over student IDs or EXISTS with the correlation <code>S.sid = E.sid</code>.</p><p>For a comparison operator op, <code>op ANY</code> requires a true comparison with at least one subquery row; <code>op ALL</code> requires it for every row. IN corresponds to = ANY, and NOT IN to &lt;&gt; ALL. NOT EXISTS tests that no row satisfies the subquery.</p><p>SQL predicates can also evaluate to UNKNOWN because of NULL. WHERE retains only TRUE rows. Consequently NOT IN and NOT EXISTS need not behave identically when NULL is possible. On an empty subquery, EXISTS and ANY are false, while NOT EXISTS and ALL are true.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 26–29</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In Sale, what is Hue’s total after WHERE amount >= 30?","options":["130","120","90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The row with amount=10 is filtered out, leaving 30+90=120. WHERE filters individual rows before GROUP BY computes SUM, so the sale of 10 does not contribute."},"source":{"summary":"SQL terminology and history; DDL/DML/DCL; SELECT, sorting, limits, aggregates, grouping, joins, IN/EXISTS, ALL/ANY.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=6","label":"PDF pp. 6–12 / printed slides 7–13 · tables and SELECT"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–19 / slides 14–20 · aggregates and evaluation"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pp. 20–28 / slides 21–29 · joins and nested queries"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture02-sql-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["SQL history","DDL/DML/DCL","Schema/instance","Bags","SELECT/DISTINCT","ORDER BY/LIMIT","Aggregates","GROUP BY/HAVING","Joins","Outer joins","IN/EXISTS","ALL/ANY"],"limitations":["Three-valued NULL logic is not taught here.","Conceptual SQL order is not physical execution order."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"SQL","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"SQL H ISTORY"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"SQL’ S P ERSISTENCE"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"SQL P ROS AND C ONS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R ELATIONAL T ERMINOLOGY"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"R ELATIONAL TABLES"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"SQL L ANGUAGE"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"E XAMPLE D ATABASE"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"B ASIC S INGLE -TABLE Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"O RDER B Y                                 ORDER BY <column*> [ASC|DESC]"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"L IMIT                                                   LIMIT <count> [offset]"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"A GGREGATES"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"G ROUP B Y"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"G ROUP B Y"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"F ILTER G ROUPS"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"H AVING"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"C ONCEPTUAL SQL E VALUATION"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"M ULTIPLE -TABLE Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"J OIN Q UERY"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"J OIN Q UERY – A NOTHER S YNTAX"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"J OIN VARIANTS"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"L EFT O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"R IGHT O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"F ULL O UTER J OIN"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"N ESTED Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"N ESTED Q UERIES"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"M ORE ON S ET-C OMPARISON O PERATORS"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"sql"},{"id":"lecture-03","lecture":3,"title":"Relational algebra","short":"Relational algebra","group":"01 · Foundations","time":25,"lead":"From declarative SQL to relational operators, set semantics and composed query plans.","goals":["Query languages and relational algebra","Selection and projection","Union, difference, product and renaming"],"sections":[{"id":"l3-1","title":"Query languages and relational algebra","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l3-1\"></span><p>SQL describes a required result; relational algebra describes transformations of relations. A DBMS translates the query into a logical plan, applies equivalence-preserving rewrites and selects physical operators. Codd’s theorem connects relational algebra with the domain-independent relational calculus.</p><p>Each relational-algebra operator takes relation instances and returns a relation instance. This closure property allows operators to be composed into a query tree. Input schemas determine output schemas and allow invalid expressions to be rejected before execution.</p><p>Pure relational algebra uses sets, so a relation contains no duplicate tuples. SQL uses bags by default. When moving between them, duplicate elimination must be accounted for explicitly.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–7</a></p>"},{"id":"l3-2","title":"Selection and projection","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l3-2\"></span><p>Selection <code>σ<sub>p</sub>(R)</code> keeps exactly the tuples of R satisfying predicate p. It changes the rows but keeps the input schema. Predicates can combine conditions with conjunction and disjunction.</p><p>Projection <code>π<sub>A</sub>(R)</code> keeps the attributes listed in A. Its output schema is that attribute list. Under set semantics, tuples that become identical after projection collapse into one tuple. SQL SELECT without DISTINCT normally retains their multiplicities.</p><p>Selection and projection therefore operate on different dimensions of a relation: selection restricts tuples, while projection restricts attributes. Their position in a composed expression determines which attributes must remain available to later operators.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 8–9</a></p>"},{"id":"l3-3","title":"Union, difference, product and renaming","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l3-3\"></span><p>Union R ∪ S and difference R − S require compatible schemas: the same number of attributes and compatible types at corresponding positions. Union contains tuples in either input; difference contains tuples in R that are absent from S. Difference is not commutative. Intersection can be expressed as <code>R − (R − S)</code>.</p><p>The Cartesian product R × S pairs every tuple of R with every tuple of S, yielding |R|·|S| tuples. The output contains attributes from both relations. Renaming ρ supplies relation or attribute names where names would otherwise collide.</p><p>SQL UNION, EXCEPT and INTERSECT use duplicate elimination. Their ALL variants use bag multiplicities: union adds counts, intersection takes their minimum, and difference takes max(mR − mS, 0). SQLite does not implement EXCEPT ALL or INTERSECT ALL.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 10–14</a></p>"},{"id":"l3-4","title":"Joins and additional operators","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l3-4\"></span><p>A theta join is <code>R ⋈<sub>θ</sub> S = σ<sub>θ</sub>(R × S)</code>. An equijoin uses equality predicates. A natural join equates every shared attribute name and projects away the duplicate copies of those attributes. A self-join uses two renamed references to the same relation.</p><p>The product-and-selection definition specifies the result, not a requirement to materialise the product. Physical join algorithms compute the same result more efficiently.</p><p>Extended algebra includes grouping and aggregation γ, duplicate elimination δ, sorting τ, assignment and division. A logical plan specifies these relational operations; a physical plan additionally selects implementations such as an index scan or sort-merge join.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 15–20</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"If R has 3 rows and S has 4 rows, how many rows does R × S contain?","options":["7","12","At most 4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each R row combines with every S row: 3×4=12. The Cartesian product has not yet applied a join condition; a later selection may reduce the number of pairs."},"source":{"summary":"Query compilation and Codd’s theorem; compositional relational algebra, set semantics, core operators, joins and additional operators.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–7 / slides 2–7 · plans, calculus, and semantics"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pp. 8–18 / slides 8–18 · operators"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pp. 19–20 / slides 19–20 · additional operators"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture03-ra-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–20","pdfPageCount":20,"topics":["Query execution","Calculus/RA","Codd theorem","Closure/types","Sets/bags","Selection","Projection","Union/difference","Product/rename","Intersection","Theta/equi/natural join","Extra operators"],"limitations":["Division is listed, not developed.","Do not reproduce slide 11 erroneous S−R table."],"week":1,"officialTitle":"Relational Algebra","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-1","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"Q UERY E XECUTION O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"Q UERY E XECUTION O VERVIEW"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"R ELATIONAL Q UERY L ANGUAGES"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"C ODD ’ S T HEOREM"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"R ELATIONAL A LGEBRA"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R ELATIONAL A LGEBRA AND S ETS"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"S ELECTION"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"P ROJECTION"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"U NION                 R     S                R∪S"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"S ET D IFFERENCE                        R     S       R –S       S"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"C ROSS P RODUCT"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"R ENAMING (⍴ = “rho”)"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"C OMPOUND O PERATOR : I NTERSECTION"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"C OMPOUND O PERATOR : J OIN"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"T HETA J OIN E XAMPLE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"T HETA J OIN E XAMPLE 2"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"N ATURAL J OIN"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"E XTRA O PERATORS"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":5},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-04","lecture":4,"title":"Hardware and disk space management","short":"Hardware and disk space management","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"DBMS layers, storage hierarchy, page I/O and the disk space manager.","goals":["DBMS architecture","Storage hierarchy and magnetic disks","SSDs and page-oriented storage"],"sections":[{"id":"l4-1","title":"DBMS architecture","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l4-1\"></span><p>The query-planning layer parses and checks SQL, then chooses an executable plan. Operator execution runs the plan over records. File and index management organises those records into pages and access structures.</p><p>Buffer management moves pages between persistent storage and memory. Disk space management maps logical page requests to physical storage operations. Each layer exposes an interface and hides the representation below it.</p><p>Concurrency control and recovery cross these layers. They coordinate simultaneous operations and restore a valid database after failures. Their correctness depends on how memory changes, page writes and log writes are ordered.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–10</a></p>"},{"id":"l4-2","title":"Storage hierarchy and magnetic disks","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l4-2\"></span><p>Disk-oriented DBMSs keep the database on non-volatile storage and process data in volatile memory. Registers, caches and DRAM offer low latency but limited capacity; SSDs and HDDs provide larger persistent storage. A page read or write transfers a block of bytes, rather than an individual field.</p><p>For an HDD, access time has three components: seek time, rotational delay and transfer time. The slide’s 8 KB example sums 3.40 ms, 2.00 ms and approximately 0.05 ms to obtain 5.45 ms. These are parameters of that device example.</p><p>Random reads repeatedly pay seek and rotational overhead. Sequential reads amortise that overhead across neighbouring blocks. Physical placement and prefetching therefore affect scan performance.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 11–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l4-3","title":"SSDs and page-oriented storage","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l4-3\"></span><p>An SSD removes mechanical seek and rotation, but reads, writes and erasures still have different granularities and costs. NAND flash is erased in blocks larger than the read/write page. Small updates can cause write amplification; wear levelling distributes erase activity.</p><p>Device bandwidth, endurance and price figures in the slides describe the cited hardware context. The design conclusion is that locality and I/O volume matter on both SSDs and HDDs, even though their penalties differ.</p><p>A database file consists of pages containing records. The DBMS seeks spatial control over placement and temporal control over transfers. Reducing the number of I/Os and using more useful data per I/O are central goals.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–25</a></p>"},{"id":"l4-4","title":"Disk space manager","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l4-4\"></span><p>The disk space manager allocates and deallocates pages, reads pages into memory and writes pages back. Each page has a logical page ID. The layer maintains the mapping from that identifier to a physical file and byte offset.</p><p>Higher layers can request a sequence of pages. The manager aims to make consecutive logical pages efficient to scan by arranging their physical locations appropriately. A logical database file may span several operating-system files or devices.</p><p>This interface hides filesystem and device details from buffer management. “Next page” expresses an intended locality property; actual performance still depends on allocation and the storage system.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 26–29</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Under the exercise’s model, how long does reading 200 contiguous pages take?","options":["1420 ms","27 ms","20 ms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Seek time plus rotational latency is 7 ms. Transferring 200 pages takes 20 ms, giving a total of 27 ms under the stated assumptions."},"source":{"summary":"Layered DBMS architecture; storage hierarchy; HDD/SSD; random versus sequential access; page abstraction and disk-space allocation/mapping.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–14 / slides 2–14 · architecture and the memory hierarchy"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pp. 15–23 / slides 15–24 · devices and I/O"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf#page=24","label":"PDF pp. 24–29 / slides 25–31 · the disk-space manager"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture04-hardware-disk-space-management-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["DBMS layers","Concurrency/recovery overview","Volatility","Hierarchy","HDD anatomy","Seek/rotation/transfer","Sequential locality","SSD/wear levelling","Pages","Allocation","Page-ID mapping"],"limitations":["Hardware figures are dated examples, not current purchasing advice."],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Hardware & Disk Space Management","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"DBMS: B IG P ICTURE"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"DBMS: Q UERY P LANNING"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"DBMS: O PERATOR E XECUTION"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"DBMS: F ILES & I NDEX M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"DBMS: B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"DBMS: D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"A RCHITECTURE OF A DBMS"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"DBMS: C ONCURRENCY & R ECOVERY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"D ISK -O RIENTED A RCHITECTURE"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"W HY N OT S TORE A LL IN M AIN M EMORY ?"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"S TORAGE H IERARCHY"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"S TORAGE H IERARCHY                           Latency   Capacity"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"A NATOMY OF A D ISK"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"A CCESSING A D ISK PAGE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Seagate Cheetah 15K.7                                           17"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"S EQUENTIAL VS . R ANDOM A CCESS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"A RRANGING B LOCKS ON D ISK"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S OLID S TATE D RIVES"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"S OLID S TATE D RIVES"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"SSD VS . HDD"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"B OTTOM L INE"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"D ATABASE S TORAGE"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"S YSTEM D ESIGN G OALS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R EQUESTS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"D ISK S PACE M ANAGEMENT : I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-05","lecture":5,"title":"Buffer management","short":"Buffer management","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"Buffer-pool state, pin/unpin, replacement policies and DBMS control over page transfers.","goals":["Buffer pool and pin/unpin","LRU and CLOCK","Sequential flooding and MRU"],"sections":[{"id":"l5-1","title":"Buffer pool and pin/unpin","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l5-1\"></span><p>A buffer pool is a memory cache divided into frames, each holding a disk page. Metadata records the page ID, dirty flag and pin count for each frame. An in-memory lookup structure maps page IDs to frames.</p><code>pin(page)</code> ensures the requested page is resident and increments its pin count. On a miss it selects a free frame or an unpinned victim, writes a dirty victim if necessary, reads the new page and returns its memory address. A frame with pin count greater than zero cannot be evicted.<code>unpin(page, dirty)</code> decrements the pin count and combines the supplied dirty flag with the existing flag using logical OR. Unpinning does not require immediate write-back. Pinning protects residency; concurrency control separately coordinates conflicting access, and recovery constrains write ordering.<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–11</a></p>"},{"id":"l5-2","title":"LRU and CLOCK","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l5-2\"></span><p>A replacement policy chooses among unpinned frames. In the slides’ LRU policy, the manager tracks the last-unpin time and evicts the eligible frame with the oldest time. Maintaining exact recency can be expensive at high page-access rates.</p><p>CLOCK approximates recency with a reference bit per frame and a circular hand. An access sets the bit. During victim search, pinned frames are skipped; an unpinned frame with bit 1 has its bit cleared and receives another chance; an unpinned frame with bit 0 can be replaced. The hand advances as frames are examined.</p><p>The source trace replaces page 8 with page 10 after skipping pinned and recently referenced frames. Dirty-page write-back is a separate action from selecting the victim.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 12–21</a></p>"},{"id":"l5-3","title":"Sequential flooding and MRU","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l5-3\"></span><p>Sequential flooding occurs when a scan displaces pages that will be needed again before the scan ends. The source compares repeated scans of a six-page and a seven-page relation using six initially empty frames.</p><p>With six pages, LRU incurs six initial misses and then hits. With seven pages, requesting page 7 evicts page 1; the next pass requests page 1 and evicts page 2. This pattern continues, making every request a miss under the stated immediate-release scan assumptions.</p><p>MRU evicts the most recently used eligible page. In the source’s repeated-scan case, it preserves pages that will be reused earlier in the next pass. LRU, MRU and LRU-K exploit different access patterns; no policy is optimal for every workload.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 22–31</a></p>"},{"id":"l5-4","title":"Practical buffer management","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l5-4\"></span><p>The execution engine knows whether an access is a sequential scan, an index lookup or a repeated join input. It can provide priority hints, separate buffer pools for different page types and avoid treating every reference identically.</p><p>Prefetching requests upcoming pages before they are demanded. It can amortise storage overhead and overlap computation with I/O. Its usefulness depends on predicting pages that will actually be consumed.</p><p>The DBMS retains control rather than relying entirely on the operating-system cache because recovery requires controlled flushing and ordering, while query plans provide information useful to replacement and prefetching.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 32–35</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"P has dirty=true. What is dirty after unpin(P,false)?","options":["false","true","It depends on the pin count"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The new dirty flag is the old dirty flag OR the supplied dirty argument. true OR false remains true."},"source":{"summary":"Buffer metadata and pin/unpin; LRU/CLOCK/MRU; sequential flooding; LRU-K; priority hints, partitioned pools, prefetch and OS interaction.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pp. 3–11 / slides 3–11 · buffer state"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–21 / slides 14–22 · LRU/CLOCK"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf#page=22","label":"PDF pp. 22–35 / slides 26–39 · scans, MRU, and practical considerations"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture05-buffer-management-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–35","pdfPageCount":35,"topics":["Frames/page table","Pin/unpin","Dirty writeback","LRU","CLOCK","Sequential flooding","MRU","LRU-K","Priority hints","Partitioned pools","Prefetch","OS buffering"],"limitations":["Practical DBMS claims in slides are course context, not a current-version implementation guarantee."],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Buffer Management","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R EQUEST"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT : PAGE R ELEASE"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"O PEN Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGER S TATE"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"P ROPER P IN /U NPIN N ESTING"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"P IN I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"U NPIN I MPLEMENTATION"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"A DVANCED Q UESTIONS"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"B UFFER R EPLACEMENT P OLICIES"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"L EAST R ECENTLY U SED (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"L EAST R ECENTLY U SED (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"C LOCK R EPLACEMENT P OLICY                                             p1"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY S TATE : E XPLICIT & I LLUSTRATED"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"C LOCK P OLICY : R EAD PAGE 10 (C ONT.)"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"R EPLACEMENT P OLICIES C AN F AIL"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (LRU)"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":32,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":33,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":34,"heading":"R EPEATED S CAN (MRU)"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":35,"heading":"B EST R EPLACEMENT P OLICY ?"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":36,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":37,"heading":"B UFFER M ANAGEMENT IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":38,"heading":"W HY N OT U SE THE OS?"},{"pdfPage":35,"printedSlide":39,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":"buffer"},{"id":"lecture-06","lecture":6,"title":"Files, pages and records","short":"Files, pages and records","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"File organisation, fixed and variable-length pages, slotted pages and record representation.","goals":["Database files and heap files","Fixed-length page layouts","Variable-length records and slotted pages"],"sections":[{"id":"l6-1","title":"Database files and heap files","html":"<p>A logical database file is a collection of pages containing records. The file layer supports creating files, inserting/deleting/updating records, fetching a record by RID and scanning records. A logical file can span operating-system files or machines.</p><p>Heap files store records without a key order. They must track their pages, free space and record locations. One implementation maintains separate linked lists of full pages and pages with free space; searching for enough space may visit many pages.</p><p>A page directory stores page identifiers and free-space information in compact directory pages. A cached directory can locate a suitable data page with fewer reads. Sorted files and index-organised files offer other access/update trade-offs.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–9</a></p>"},{"id":"l6-2","title":"Fixed-length page layouts","html":"<p>A page header records metadata such as the page ID, record count and free space. With fixed-length packed records, position i starts at <code>headerSize + (i − 1)·recordSize</code>. Insertion appends after the current last record.</p><p>Deleting a packed record can fill its gap with the last record, but this changes that moved record’s position-based RID. Any references to that RID may need updating.</p><p>An unpacked layout leaves empty slots. A bitmap marks occupied positions, or a free list links available slots. Insertion reuses a free slot and deletion releases one, so unrelated records need not move. The layout trades metadata and unused space for stable positions.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 10–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l6-3","title":"Variable-length records and slotted pages","html":"<p>Variable-length records cannot be located using one constant record size. A slotted page instead maintains a directory mapping slot IDs to current record offsets. Its RID is <code>(page ID, slot ID)</code>.</p><p>The slot directory and records grow from opposite parts of the page. Insertion uses an available slot or creates one and allocates enough bytes for the record. Deletion invalidates the slot; compaction can move remaining record bytes and update their directory offsets.</p><p>Moving bytes within a page does not change a record’s RID while its slot ID remains unchanged. This indirection allows compaction without rewriting external references to every moved record. Reusing a deleted slot still requires the DBMS’s normal record-lifecycle rules.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–23</a></p>"},{"id":"l6-4","title":"Record layout and NULL representation","html":"<p>Field types belong to the schema stored in the system catalog, so each record need not repeat its complete schema. Fixed-length fields can be addressed by offsets computed from the field sizes.</p><p>Variable-length fields can use delimiters or an offset array. Delimiters require scanning and escaping; offsets support direct field access. Another layout keeps fixed-size offset/length descriptors near fixed-length values and stores variable data afterwards.</p><p>NULL requires explicit metadata, commonly a bitmap. An empty variable-length field and a NULL field cannot always be distinguished by offsets alone. The source’s byte-layout figure combines numeric fields, string descriptors, string bytes and a NULL bitmap.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 24–29</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In the slotted-page example, must the index change the RID when the record in slot 2 moves?","options":["Yes, always","No, if the slot ID remains unchanged","The RID is a byte offset, so the record cannot move"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slot directory updates the byte offset. The index still finds the record through (page 9, slot 2)."},"source":{"summary":"Files/records API; heap page lists and directories; fixed packed/unpacked layouts; slotted pages; fixed/variable fields and NULL metadata.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=4","label":"PDF pp. 4–9 / slides 4–9 · files and heaps"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=11","label":"PDF pp. 11–22 / slides 11–22 · page layout"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf#page=24","label":"PDF pp. 24–29 / slides 24–29 · record bytes"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture06-file-pages-records-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–29","pdfPageCount":29,"topics":["Logical file/API","Heap linked list","Page directory","Packed records","Bitmap/free list","Slotted page","RID","Fixed fields","Variable fields","NULL bitmap"],"limitations":[],"week":2,"officialTitle":"Files, Pages & Records","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-2","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"O VERVIEW : R EPRESENTATIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"F ILES OF PAGES OF R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"F ILES OF PAGES OF R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"DB F ILE O RGANISATION"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"H EAP F ILE"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : L INKED L IST"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : PAGE D IRECTORY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"PAGE L AYOUT"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , P ACKED"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (B ITMAP )"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (F REE L IST )"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS , U NPACKED (F REE L IST )"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"S LOTTED PAGES"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"S LOTTED PAGES"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"O UTLINE"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"R ECORD L AYOUT"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"R ECORD L AYOUT : F IXED -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"VARIABLE -L ENGTH R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-07","lecture":7,"title":"Storage models and compression","short":"Storage models and compression","group":"02 · Data storage","time":25,"lead":"OLTP/OLAP workloads, row and column storage, PAX, Parquet and lossless encoding.","goals":["Workloads and physical storage models","Row storage and column storage","Hybrid storage and Parquet"],"sections":[{"id":"l7-1","title":"Workloads and physical storage models","html":"<span id=\"f11-layout-and-pax\"></span><p>OLTP serves many short transactions that read or change small amounts of operational data. OLAP serves complex analytical queries scanning large amounts of data and computing aggregates. HTAP combines transactional and analytical processing on the same database.</p><p>The relational model specifies logical relations; it does not require all attributes of a tuple to occupy the same physical page. A storage model specifies how tuple values are arranged on disk and in memory.</p><p>Row storage, column storage and hybrid layouts make different choices about locality. Their suitability depends on whether queries need complete individual tuples or a few attributes from many tuples, and on the frequency of updates.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l7-2","title":"Row storage and column storage","html":"<span id=\"f11-column-position-exercise\"></span><p>Row storage places a tuple’s attributes together. Reading or updating one complete tuple has good locality, which suits OLTP. A scan using only a few columns nevertheless reads bytes for unrelated attributes.</p><p>Column storage places values of one attribute together. It reads only required columns, improves locality for scans and groups similar values for compression. Tuple reconstruction combines corresponding positions across columns.</p><p>The trade-off is that a point query or update may access several separate column structures. Variable-length values and NULLs also need suitable representations, such as dictionary codes and metadata arrays. Physical layout changes performance without changing the logical relation.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7–16</a></p>"},{"id":"l7-3","title":"Hybrid storage and Parquet","html":"<span id=\"f13-value-aware-compression\"></span><p>PAX partitions a page or row group into separate regions for its attributes. Related rows remain within one group while each attribute region supports column-oriented processing and compression.</p><p>The source describes Parquet as row groups containing column chunks, which contain pages. Metadata records schemas, offsets, encodings and statistics; the file footer locates row groups. Repetition and definition levels support nested data.</p><p>Reading selected column chunks avoids unnecessary attributes. Min/max and other statistics can skip regions that cannot satisfy a predicate. Row groups also provide units for parallel processing. These are layout and metadata mechanisms; their benefit depends on the query and data distribution.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=17\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 17–20</a></p>"},{"id":"l7-4","title":"Lossless compression techniques","html":"<span id=\"f12-parquet-metadata\"></span><span id=\"f13-compression-performance\"></span><p>Database compression must preserve exact values. It reduces storage, memory use and I/O, but encoding/decoding consumes CPU time. General-purpose block compression often requires decompression before values can be accessed.</p><p>Run-length encoding stores repeated consecutive values with their counts. Delta encoding stores differences between consecutive values. Bit packing uses only the bits needed for a bounded integer range. These methods can be combined when their assumptions fit the data.</p><p>Dictionary encoding maps distinct values to compact codes and keeps the reverse mapping. It is useful for repeated strings and can permit some operations directly on codes. Ordered comparisons require an order-preserving dictionary or comparison of decoded values. The Parquet delta example illustrates grouping and packing differences; it does not establish one universal compression ratio.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 21–28</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What is the minimum number of bits per unsigned value needed to encode residual=[2,0,3,0]?","options":["1","2","3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two unsigned bits have four patterns, 00,01,10,11, representing 0 through 3. The residual maximum is 3, so two bits suffice; one bit would represent only 0 and 1. This counts residual payload, not the first value, minimum delta or format metadata."},"source":{"summary":"OLTP/OLAP/HTAP; row/column/PAX; Parquet organisation and metadata; lossless compression trade-offs; RLE, delta, bit packing, dictionary.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–16 / slides 2–17 · workloads and storage"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=17","label":"PDF pp. 17–20 / slides 18–21 · PAX/Parquet"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–28 / slides 22–29 · compression"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture07-storage-models-compression-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–28","pdfPageCount":28,"topics":["OLTP/OLAP/HTAP","Row store","Column store","PAX","Parquet hierarchy","Metadata pruning","Compression tradeoff","Generic codecs","RLE","Delta/min-delta","Bit packing","Dictionary"],"limitations":["Parquet exercise is conceptual, not a specification-conformant implementation.","Printed slide 16 is absent; PDF page 16 is slide 17."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"Storage Models & Compression","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"DATABASE W ORKLOADS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"OLTP: ON-LINE TRANSACTIONAL PROCESSING"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"OLAP: O N -L INE A NALYTICAL P ROCESSING"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"O BSERVATION"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"S TORAGE M ODELS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"R OW S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"COLUMNAR S TORAGE M ODEL"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"H YBRID S TORAGE M ODEL (PAX)"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"H YBRID S TORAGE M ODEL                                              Col A      Col B      Col C"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"PARQUET F ILE F ORMAT"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"PARQUET F ILE F ORMAT"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"COMPRESSION IN DBMS"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"N AÏVE COMPRESSION"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"COLUMNAR COMPRESSION"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"D ELTA E NCODING IN PARQUET"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"D ICTIONARY E NCODING"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"CONCLUSION"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":7},"lab":"storage"},{"id":"lecture-08","lecture":8,"title":"File organisations","short":"File organisations","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"I/O cost models for heap and sorted files, index entries and clustering.","goals":["Cost model and heap files","Sorted files","Index search keys and entry variants"],"sections":[{"id":"l8-1","title":"Cost model and heap files","html":"<p>Let P be the number of data pages, R the records per page and D the average time per page I/O. The source model ignores CPU, prefetching and the difference between sequential and random I/O. Its estimates compare access methods under explicit simplifying assumptions.</p><p>A heap scan costs PD. An equality search for one existing uniformly located unique key reads about P/2 pages on average; an unsuccessful search or a non-key search must inspect the whole file. Range search also scans all pages.</p><p>With append-at-end insertion, reading and writing the last page costs 2D. Deleting one existing record costs approximately (P/2 + 1)D: locate it and write its page. These formulas exclude additional allocation and directory costs.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–13</a></p>"},{"id":"l8-2","title":"Sorted files","html":"<span id=\"f14-file-cost-completion\"></span><p>A sorted file orders records by a search key. A full scan still costs PD. Searching a matching sort key can use binary search, requiring approximately log₂P page reads. Range search locates the first match and then scans the matching region.</p><p>If q pages contain matching records, the source estimates range access as (log₂P + q)D. Binary search is useful only when the predicate is compatible with the file’s order.</p><p>Insertion and deletion in the compact sorted-file model shift subsequent records. On average P/2 pages are read and rewritten, adding approximately PD after locating the position. Sorted files therefore favour ordered retrieval over frequent changes.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 14–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l8-3","title":"Index search keys and entry variants","html":"<span id=\"f14-index-classification\"></span><p>An index maps search-key values to data entries. A search key can be any chosen attribute list and need not uniquely identify a record. This differs from a relational candidate key.</p><p>Variant A stores the record itself as the data entry. Variant B stores (key, RID). Variant C stores (key, list of RIDs), grouping references with the same key. B and C permit several indexes over the same underlying records without duplicating full tuples.</p><p>Tree indexes preserve key order and support equality and ranges. Hash indexes distribute keys to buckets and suit equality lookup. Index entries, index navigation pages and heap data pages are distinct objects whose I/O costs must be counted separately.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–29</a></p>"},{"id":"l8-4","title":"Clustered and unclustered indexes","html":"<p>Clustering means that the physical order of data records follows, or approximately follows, the index key order. A heap file can have only one physical clustering order, while several unclustered indexes can reference it.</p><p>For a range, a clustered index often fetches each matching data page once. An unclustered index can require a page fetch per matching record when references alternate between pages and buffering does not retain them. This is why the same index selectivity can have very different retrieval costs.</p><p>Clustering improves locality and ordered scans but costs work to maintain. Free space, overflow pages and later reorganisation accommodate inserts. The best file organisation depends on the workload’s balance of scans, point lookups, ranges and updates.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=30\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 30–34</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"The 80 matching records occupy four data pages. How many page reads fetch those records if none of the pages is cached?","options":["4, excluding index traversal","Always 80","0, because an index exists"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Fetch each of the four required data pages once; all 80 matching records are on those pages. Several matching records share a page, so record count is not page-read count. This excludes the separately charged index path and assumes the pages are not cached."},"source":{"summary":"I/O-only file cost model; heap/sorted operations; index entry variants; search keys; tree/hash; clustering, primary/secondary and uniqueness.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=5","label":"PDF pp. 5–19 / slides 5–21 · cost model"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pp. 20–29 / slides 22–33 · index concepts"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf#page=30","label":"PDF pp. 30–34 / slides 36–49 · clustering"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture08-file-organisations-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–34","pdfPageCount":34,"topics":["P/R/D assumptions","Heap costs","Sorted costs","Range search","Insert/delete","Search key","Entry variants A/B/C","Tree/hash","Primary/secondary","Unique","Clustered/unclustered"],"limitations":["Printed slide numbers have gaps; final PDF page 34 is printed slide 49.","Costs are simplified approximations under stated assumptions."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"File Organisations","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"R ECAP : F ILE O RGANISATIONS"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"C OMPARING F ILE O RGANISATIONS"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"G OALS"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"C OST M ODEL FOR A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"R ECORD O PERATIONS"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"H EAP F ILES"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S CAN A LL R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S EARCH ON K EY"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : S EARCH ON N ON -K EY"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : R ANGE S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"H EAP F ILE : I NSERT & D ELETE"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"H EAP F ILE A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE : I NSERT & D ELETE"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"S ORTED F ILE A NALYSIS"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"H EAP F ILE VS . S ORTED F ILE"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"I NTRODUCTION TO I NDEXES"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"I NDEX U SAGE"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"I NDEX E NTRIES"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"I NDEX E NTRIES"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"I NDEXING BY R EFERENCE"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"I NDEX C LASSIFICATION"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":31,"heading":"B+ T REE I NDEXES"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":32,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":33,"heading":"H ASH -B ASED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":36,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":31,"printedSlide":37,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":32,"printedSlide":39,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":33,"printedSlide":40,"heading":"C LUSTERED VS . U NCLUSTERED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":34,"printedSlide":49,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":9},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-09","lecture":9,"title":"Tree-structured indexing","short":"Tree-structured indexing","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"ISAM, B+ tree invariants, search, insertion, deletion and variable-length entries.","goals":["Multilevel indexes and ISAM","B+ tree structure and lookup","Insertion and splitting"],"sections":[{"id":"l9-1","title":"Multilevel indexes and ISAM","html":"<p>A page-sized index node has high fan-out: each separator directs a search to one child page. Repeated index levels reduce the search space by a factor F per level, giving a height proportional to log<sub>F</sub>N rather than binary search’s log₂N page probes.</p><p>ISAM fixes its non-leaf structure when built. Leaf pages initially hold sorted entries; later insertions use the relevant primary leaf or its overflow chain. Deletion can remove entries without changing the non-leaf separators.</p><p>This static structure simplifies access to upper levels, but long overflow chains degrade performance as data changes. ISAM is most suitable when updates are limited.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l9-2","title":"B+ tree structure and lookup","html":"<p>A B+ tree is balanced: all leaves are at the same depth. Non-leaf keys route searches; leaves contain data entries and are linked for ordered scans. Equality lookup descends to one leaf; range lookup finds its starting leaf and follows leaf links.</p><p>In the source’s order-d convention, a non-root node holds d to 2d entries, and an internal node with m keys has m + 1 children. The root has special occupancy rules. High fan-out usually keeps the tree shallow, and upper levels may remain in the buffer pool.</p><p>A separator need not be an existing data entry after subsequent deletions; its essential role is to maintain correct child key ranges. Data entries themselves are checked at the leaves.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–16</a></p>"},{"id":"l9-3","title":"Insertion and splitting","html":"<span id=\"f15-internal-node-repair\"></span><p>Insertion finds the target leaf and inserts in key order. If the leaf overflows, its entries are split between two leaves and a separator is <strong>copied up</strong> to the parent. The leaf key remains in the leaf because leaves contain the data entries.</p><p>If the parent overflows, an internal-node split <strong>pushes up</strong> the middle separator: it becomes the parent’s routing key and is removed from the split node. Child pointers must be redistributed with the keys. Splitting the root creates a new root and increases the height.</p><p>The source insertion of 8* demonstrates both cases: 5 is copied up from the leaf and 17 is pushed up from the internal node. Redistribution to a non-full sibling can sometimes avoid a split, but requires adjusting the parent separator.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=17\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 17–20</a></p>"},{"id":"l9-4","title":"Deletion, occupancy and key compression","html":"<span id=\"f16-occupancy-conventions\"></span><span id=\"f16-isam-and-key-compression\"></span><p>After deletion, an underfull node first attempts redistribution with an adjacent sibling sharing its parent. The parent separator must reflect the new boundary. If redistribution is impossible, merge the nodes and remove the corresponding parent entry. Underflow can propagate to the root.</p><p>For internal-node merging, the parent separator is pulled down between the two groups of keys and pointers. An empty root can be removed, reducing tree height. Some practical implementations relax minimum occupancy and reclaim only empty pages.</p><p>Variable-length entries require byte-based occupancy rather than a fixed entry count. Prefix compression reduces repeated leaf-key prefixes; suffix truncation shortens routing keys while preserving correct comparisons. These optimisations must retain the tree’s search invariants.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 21–30</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"When the leaf [4,9,12,15,18] splits into [4,9] and [12,15,18], what happens to separator 12?","options":["It is removed from every leaf","It is copied into the parent and retained in the right leaf","It is retained only in the left leaf"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The right leaf starts at 12, so its parent boundary is 12. Copy that boundary into the parent but retain the data entry in the right leaf: a leaf-chain scan must still return 12. Internal-node splitting instead moves a separator up while preserving the leaf data."},"source":{"summary":"ISAM and overflow; fanout; B+ tree search and invariants; leaf/inner splits; redistribution/merge; deletion practice; variable-length occupancy and key compression.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pp. 2–12 / slides 2–12 · ISAM/fanout"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pp. 13–20 / slides 13–20 · B+ tree search and insertion"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pp. 21–29 / slides 21–29 · deletion and optimisations"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing.pdf","alternatePdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture09-tree-indexing-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–30","pdfPageCount":30,"topics":["Sorted/binary search","Multilevel index","ISAM overflow","Fanout","B+ invariants","Range leaf links","Leaf copy-up","Inner push-up","Redistribution","Merge/root collapse","Deletion in practice","Byte occupancy","Prefix/suffix compression"],"limitations":["Order d is the lecture convention; other materials define order differently.","Tree simulator examples enforce textbook invariants, unlike some practical lazy-deletion policies."],"week":3,"officialTitle":"Tree-Structured Indexing","weeklyPage":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/week-3","pageMap":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedSlide":null,"heading":"Advanced Database Systems"},{"pdfPage":2,"printedSlide":2,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES AND B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":3,"printedSlide":3,"heading":"S ORTED F ILES AND B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":4,"printedSlide":4,"heading":"T REE -S TRUCTURED I NDEXING"},{"pdfPage":5,"printedSlide":5,"heading":"T REE -S TRUCTURED I NDEXING"},{"pdfPage":6,"printedSlide":6,"heading":"ISAM: I NDEXED S EQUENTIAL A CCESS M ETHOD"},{"pdfPage":7,"printedSlide":7,"heading":"ISAM: I NDEXED S EQUENTIAL A CCESS M ETHOD"},{"pdfPage":8,"printedSlide":8,"heading":"E XAMPLE ISAM T REE"},{"pdfPage":9,"printedSlide":9,"heading":"A FTER I NSERTING 23*, 48*, 41*, 42*…"},{"pdfPage":10,"printedSlide":10,"heading":"… T HEN D ELETING 42*, 97*, 51*"},{"pdfPage":11,"printedSlide":11,"heading":"C OMMENTS ON ISAM"},{"pdfPage":12,"printedSlide":12,"heading":"ISAM VS . B INARY S EARCH"},{"pdfPage":13,"printedSlide":13,"heading":"B+ T REE : M OST W IDELY U SED I NDEX"},{"pdfPage":14,"printedSlide":14,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":15,"printedSlide":15,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":16,"printedSlide":16,"heading":"B+ T REES IN P RACTICE"},{"pdfPage":17,"printedSlide":17,"heading":"I NSERTING A D ATA E NTRY"},{"pdfPage":18,"printedSlide":18,"heading":"I NSERTING 8* INTO E XAMPLE B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":19,"printedSlide":19,"heading":"E XAMPLE B+ T REE A FTER I NSERTING 8*"},{"pdfPage":20,"printedSlide":20,"heading":"R EDISTRIBUTION : I NSERTING 6*"},{"pdfPage":21,"printedSlide":21,"heading":"D ELETING A D ATA E NTRY FROM A B+ T REE"},{"pdfPage":22,"printedSlide":22,"heading":"D ELETING 19* AND 20*…"},{"pdfPage":23,"printedSlide":23,"heading":"… A ND T HEN D ELETING 24*"},{"pdfPage":24,"printedSlide":24,"heading":"… S TILL D ELETING 24* (M ERGE )"},{"pdfPage":25,"printedSlide":25,"heading":"… S TILL D ELETING 24* (R EDISTRIBUTION )"},{"pdfPage":26,"printedSlide":26,"heading":"B+ T REE : D ELETIONS"},{"pdfPage":27,"printedSlide":27,"heading":"VARIABLE L ENGTH K EYS & R ECORDS"},{"pdfPage":28,"printedSlide":28,"heading":"R EDEFINE O CCUPANCY I NVARIANT"},{"pdfPage":29,"printedSlide":29,"heading":"O PTIMIZATIONS"},{"pdfPage":30,"printedSlide":30,"heading":"S UMMARY"}],"alternateVerified":true,"alternatePdfPageCount":8},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-10","lecture":10,"title":"Hash-based indexing","short":"Hash-based indexing","group":"03 · Indexing","time":25,"lead":"Static hashing, collisions, extendible hashing and directory growth.","goals":["Hashing and equality lookup","Static chained hashing","Extendible hashing and depth","Bucket splits and directory doubling"],"sections":[{"id":"l10-s1","title":"Hashing and equality lookup","html":"<p>A hash function maps a search key to a bucket. Equal keys map to the same bucket, but different keys can collide, so actual keys must still be compared. Chaining and open addressing are common in-memory collision strategies.</p><p>Database hash indexes organise entries in page-sized buckets. They support equality predicates; hash order does not preserve key order and therefore does not locate an arbitrary key range efficiently. Hashing is also used for joins, grouping and duplicate elimination.</p><p>Static hashing fixes the primary bucket count. Dynamic hashing adjusts the structure as entries are inserted. The source develops extendible hashing; linear hashing is listed but not developed.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l10-s2","title":"Static chained hashing","html":"<span id=\"f17-hash-lookup-cost\"></span><p>Static chained hashing allocates N primary bucket pages. The hash function has range 0 through N − 1. A full bucket uses linked overflow pages. Search follows its bucket chain; insertion and deletion modify the appropriate page.</p><p>Without overflow, locating a bucket entry needs approximately one page read, while a modification additionally writes its page. These costs concern index entries; by-reference indexes may also need data-page fetches.</p><p>A good hash function distributes keys across buckets, but skew and growth can still create long chains. Shrinking data can leave mostly empty buckets. Rehashing restores distribution at the cost of reading and rebuilding the index.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l10-s3","title":"Extendible hashing and depth","html":"<span id=\"f17-extendible-depth\"></span><p>Extendible hashing uses a directory of bucket pointers. With global depth g, the directory has 2<sup>g</sup> entries, selected using g hash bits. The source uses least-significant bits.</p><p>Each bucket has local depth d, the number of hash bits shared by its entries. If d &lt; g, multiple directory entries point to that bucket. Lookup follows the directory pointer and compares actual keys within the bucket.</p><p>Insertion into a bucket with room changes no directory structure. When the bucket is full, the system splits that bucket and redistributes its entries using one more hash bit. Most other buckets remain untouched.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–20</a></p>"},{"id":"l10-s4","title":"Bucket splits and directory doubling","html":"<p>If a full bucket has d &lt; g, increase its local depth, split it and update the relevant directory pointers. The existing directory already distinguishes the extra bit, so its size is unchanged.</p><p>If d = g, the split requires an additional directory bit. Double the directory, increase global depth and redirect pointers for the two split buckets. Entries are redistributed only for the bucket being split. Repeated splits may be necessary if the new bit still fails to separate the keys.</p><p>The source insertion sequence grows global depth from 2 to 3. Directory growth is cheaper than redistributing every data page, but directory size and hash skew remain practical constraints.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 21–26</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A bucket with local depth 2 and global depth 4 is split into buckets with local depth 3. Which statement is correct?","options":["The directory does not need to double","The directory must double","Every bucket must be rehashed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Before the split the directory uses four bits. The split only needs three bits to distinguish the two new buckets, so the existing directory is already sufficient. The old bucket has 2^(4−2)=4 pointers; after redirection each new depth-three bucket has 2^(4−3)=2. Other buckets need not split."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: In-memory hashing and collision resolution; Static chained hashing: search, insertion, deletion, and overflow; Hash functions, skew, and rehashing; Extendible hashing: global/local depth, splitting, doubling, and shrinking. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–12"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pages 13–25"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–26"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture10-hash-indexing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–26","topics":["In-memory hashing and collision resolution","Static chained hashing: search, insertion, deletion, and overflow","Hash functions, skew, and rehashing","Extendible hashing: global/local depth, splitting, doubling, and shrinking"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-11","lecture":11,"title":"External sorting and aggregation","short":"External sorting and aggregation","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"External merge sort, ordered index scans, external hashing and grouped aggregates.","goals":["Sorting and two-way external merge sort","Multiway merge sort and index order","Duplicate elimination and external hashing","Hash aggregation and memory requirements"],"sections":[{"id":"l11-s1","title":"Sorting and two-way external merge sort","html":"<span id=\"f18-merge-values\"></span><p>Sorting supports ORDER BY, duplicate elimination, grouping and sort-merge joins. If a file exceeds available memory, an external algorithm must explicitly control page reads and writes.</p><p>Two-way external merge sort first reads, sorts and writes each individual page as a run. Each later pass merges pairs of sorted runs. One input buffer per run and one output buffer are sufficient for merging.</p><p>For N pages, this version takes 1 + ⌈log₂N⌉ passes. If each pass reads and writes all pages, its I/O cost is 2N times the pass count. This includes writing the sorted result; pipelining that result changes the accounting.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–10</a></p>"},{"id":"l11-s2","title":"Multiway merge sort and index order","html":"<p>With B buffers, pass 0 produces ⌈N/B⌉ sorted runs. A merge pass combines up to B − 1 input runs, reserving one buffer for output. The pass count is <code>1 + ⌈log<sub>B−1</sub>⌈N/B⌉⌉</code> for a nonempty input, with one initial pass when everything fits.</p><p>The slide example has N = 108 and B = 5: 22 initial runs, then 6, then 2, then 1. Four passes cost 2·108·4 = 864 page I/Os.</p><p>A clustered B+ tree can supply records in key order without a separate external sort. Following an unclustered index in key order can instead fetch one data page per record, so it must be compared with scan-and-sort.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 11–17</a></p>"},{"id":"l11-s3","title":"Duplicate elimination and external hashing","html":"<span id=\"f18-hash-state-capacity\"></span><p>Sorting places equal values consecutively, so duplicate elimination can retain one tuple from each equal group. If ordering is unnecessary, hashing can group equal values without producing a global order.</p><p>External hashing partitions the input on disk with a hash function, using one input buffer and up to B − 1 output buffers. Every occurrence of a key goes to the same partition. Each partition is then processed using an in-memory hash table and a second hash function.</p><p>The hash table, rather than just the partition’s raw bytes, must fit memory. Oversized partitions require recursive partitioning or another strategy. Skew can prevent even partition sizes.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 18–28</a></p>"},{"id":"l11-s4","title":"Hash aggregation and memory requirements","html":"<p>Hash aggregation stores a group key and running aggregate state. COUNT stores a count; SUM a sum; MIN/MAX the current extremum. AVG requires both sum and count, combined only when the group result is produced.</p><p>During partitioning, equal group keys must remain together. The rehash phase updates each group’s state rather than retaining every original tuple when the aggregate permits this. Partial aggregation can therefore reduce intermediate volume.</p><p>The source’s rough two-phase capacity is B(B − 1) pages under even distribution, adjusted for hash-table overhead by a factor f. Sorting is useful when ordered output is also needed; hashing is often preferred for unordered grouping and DISTINCT.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 29–32</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"For N=90 and B=6, how many I/Os does multiway sorting require when the final output is written?","options":["360","540","900"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pass 0 reads and writes 90 pages and creates 15 runs. Five input frames plus one output frame merge them into 3 runs, then 1. These are three complete passes, each 90 reads+90 writes=180 I/Os: 3×180=540, including the final sorted-file write."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: Query execution and operator implementations; Lexicographic sorting, two-way and multiway external merge sort; B+ tree sorting: clustered/unclustered; Sort-based DISTINCT; External hashing, recursive partitioning, and skew; Hash aggregation, running state, and memory bounds. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–32"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pages 15–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–32"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture11-sorting-aggregation_0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–32","topics":["Query execution and operator implementations","Lexicographic sorting, two-way and multiway external merge sort","B+ tree sorting: clustered/unclustered","Sort-based DISTINCT","External hashing, recursive partitioning, and skew","Hash aggregation, running state, and memory bounds"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"sorting"},{"id":"lecture-12","lecture":12,"title":"Join algorithms","short":"Join algorithms","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"Nested-loop, indexed, sort-merge and hash joins with explicit page-I/O assumptions.","goals":["Nested-loop joins","Index nested-loop join","Sort-merge join","Hash join and algorithm choice"],"sections":[{"id":"l12-s1","title":"Nested-loop joins","html":"<p>Let R have M pages and m tuples, S have N pages, and let B be the number of buffer frames. The source counts input and temporary-page I/O, ignoring CPU and final output writes equally across algorithms.</p><p>Tuple nested loops scans S for every tuple in R: <code>M + mN</code>. Page nested loops scans S for each page of R: <code>M + MN</code>. Block nested loops uses B − 2 frames for an outer block, one for inner input and one for output: <code>M + ⌈M/(B − 2)⌉N</code>.</p><p>Compute both orientations because changing outer and inner changes the cost. When the outer input fits in B − 2 frames, block nested loops reads each input once, costing M + N.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–11</a></p>"},{"id":"l12-s2","title":"Index nested-loop join","html":"<p>Index nested loops scans the outer relation and probes an index on the inner join attribute for each outer tuple. Its cost is <code>M + m·lookupCost</code>, where lookupCost includes both index navigation and fetching all matching inner records.</p><p>A clustered index groups matching records on relatively few data pages. An unclustered index can cause many random data-page fetches. A hash index supports equality; an appropriate tree index can support ordered comparisons.</p><p>The number of matches per outer key matters. A cheap index lookup does not imply a cheap join if each probe returns many records. Existing order, buffer reuse and whether the index already exists also affect the comparison.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 12–15</a></p>"},{"id":"l12-s3","title":"Sort-merge join","html":"<span id=\"f20-join-groups-and-skew\"></span><p>Sort-merge join sorts both inputs on the join key, then advances through them in key order. When keys match, it emits all matching pairs. Duplicate groups require the cross-product of the two groups, not a single output pair.</p><p>The usual merge read cost is M + N when duplicate groups can be processed without repeated disk scans. Total cost adds sorting both inputs. Large duplicate groups can require rereading or spilling; the source notes a nested-loop-like worst case.</p><p>The slide example with M = 1000, N = 500 and B = 100 costs 4000 + 2000 + 1500 = 7500 I/Os with separate sorting and merging. If the last sort passes are fused with the join and the input runs fit the buffer budget, the cost becomes 4500.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 16–33</a></p>"},{"id":"l12-s4","title":"Hash join and algorithm choice","html":"<span id=\"f19-sort-merge-fusion\"></span><p>An in-memory hash join builds a hash table on one input and probes it with the other. Matching hash values still require key comparison. It supports equijoins and must output every matching tuple combination.</p><p>Grace hash join partitions both inputs using the same join-key hash function. It then builds and probes each corresponding partition pair. One partitioning level costs <code>3(M + N)</code>: read/write both inputs, then read them for joining. The build partition’s hash table must fit memory; otherwise recursive partitioning or another strategy is needed.</p><p>Hashing benefits from a small build input; sorting benefits from existing or required order. Nested loops permits general predicates, and indexed nested loops benefits from selective probes. Costs must be compared with consistent memory and output assumptions.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 34–43</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Two groups with the same key contain 3 and 4 tuples. How many joined tuples do they produce?","options":["4","7","12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pair each of the three first-input occurrences with all four second-input occurrences: 4+4+4=12 output tuples. A join does not deduplicate the groups. Sorting or hashing can organise the comparisons differently, but cannot discard any of these valid pairs."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage: Join semantics and I/O assumptions; Tuple/page/block nested loops; Index nested loops and clustering; Sort-merge, duplicates, and final-pass fusion; In-memory hash join and Grace hash join; Recursive partitioning and algorithm trade-offs. The explanations and worked calculations above are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–43"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–15"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=16","label":"PDF pages 16–33"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf#page=34","label":"PDF pages 34–43"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture12-joins.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–43","topics":["Join semantics and I/O assumptions","Tuple/page/block nested loops","Index nested loops and clustering","Sort-merge, duplicates, and final-pass fusion","In-memory hash join and Grace hash join","Recursive partitioning and algorithm trade-offs"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-13","lecture":13,"title":"Query evaluation: processing models","short":"Query evaluation: processing models","group":"04 · Query execution","time":25,"lead":"Iterator interfaces, operator state, pipelining, vectorisation and materialisation.","goals":["Iterator interface and control flow","Stateful operator implementations","Pipelining and vectorisation","Materialisation model"],"sections":[{"id":"l13-s1","title":"Iterator interface and control flow","html":"<p>A processing model defines how operators execute a physical plan and pass results between them. In the iterator model, each operator implements open(), next() and close(), maintaining its own execution state.</p><p>The query processor opens the root and repeatedly requests its next tuple until EOF, then closes it. Operators call their children through the same interface. Control is pulled from the root towards the leaves; tuples return towards the root.</p><p>The interface allows arbitrary operator composition. A parent need not know whether its child is a scan, filter or join. LIMIT can stop requesting further tuples once enough results have been produced, subject to work already required by blocking operators.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–5</a></p>"},{"id":"l13-s2","title":"Stateful operator implementations","html":"<p>A selection’s next() repeatedly asks its child for a tuple until a predicate succeeds or EOF is reached. A heap scan tracks the current page and slot so that the next call resumes from the correct position.</p><p>A nested-loop join also remembers the current outer tuple and its position in the inner scan. After exhausting the inner input, it resets that input and advances the outer tuple. Returning one match must preserve state for the following call.</p><p>The two-pass sort example blocks during initial run generation, then its next() performs the final merge. The source assumes enough buffers to merge the generated runs in one final pass. More runs require additional merge passes.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 6–11</a></p>"},{"id":"l13-s3","title":"Pipelining and vectorisation","html":"<p>Pipelining passes tuples through successive operators without first writing the entire intermediate relation. It can reduce temporary storage and time to the first output. A sort must consume input before returning globally sorted output; a hash join must build its hash table before probing.</p><p>Tuple-at-a-time iterators incur a function call for each tuple at each operator and maintain resume state. This overhead can limit CPU efficiency even when I/O is small.</p><p>The vectorised model returns batches instead of individual tuples. It amortises calls and bookkeeping across several tuples and enables tight loops and SIMD processing. Batching changes the execution interface, not relational query semantics.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 12–15</a></p>"},{"id":"l13-s4","title":"Materialisation model","html":"<p>In the materialisation model, an operator consumes its input and produces its entire output before the parent proceeds. Evaluation moves bottom-up through the plan.</p><p>Processing complete collections can improve code and data locality, especially when intermediate results are small. Large analytical intermediates instead increase memory use and may spill to disk. “Materialisation” therefore describes a transfer boundary; whether the result resides in memory or on disk depends on its size and implementation.</p><p>The source contrasts tuple-at-a-time iterators, batch-at-a-time vectorisation and result-at-a-time materialisation. Operator blocking requirements remain relevant in each model.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 16–18</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Can one Filter.next() call invoke child.next() more than once?","options":["Yes, to discard tuples that do not satisfy the predicate","No, it always calls the child exactly once","Only when the child is an index"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The filter continues reading input until it finds a qualifying tuple or reaches EOF."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes the open/next/close iterator interface and composability; selection, heap-scan, and nested-loop state; blocking sorts and hash builds; pipelining, LIMIT, and iterator overhead; vectorized batch and SIMD processing; and materialization, execution direction, and workload trade-offs. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–9"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf#page=15","label":"PDF pages 15–18"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture13-query-evaluation-processing-models.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–18","topics":["Iterator open/next/close and composability","Selection, heap scan, nested-loop state","Blocking sort and hash build","Pipelining, LIMIT and iterator overhead","Vectorised batch/SIMD model","Materialisation, execution direction and workload trade-offs"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-14","lecture":14,"title":"Query optimisation: plan space","short":"Query optimisation: plan space","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Parsing, rewriting, algebraic equivalences, pushdown and physical alternatives.","goals":["Query optimisation and query lifecycle","Plan space and algebraic equivalences","Selection and projection pushdown","Physical plan alternatives"],"sections":[{"id":"l14-s1","title":"Query optimisation and query lifecycle","html":"<p>The optimiser seeks a correct execution plan with low estimated cost. It searches a restricted candidate space rather than guaranteeing the globally fastest program. The parser checks syntax, schemas and permissions, then constructs query blocks.</p><p>The rewriter applies semantic simplifications and algebraic equivalences. It can remove impossible or redundant predicates when the schema justifies doing so. The cost-based optimiser enumerates alternatives, estimates their costs using catalog statistics and selects a plan for execution.</p><p>This PDF has four printed slides per physical page. Page references here identify the physical PDF page; printed slide numbers are separate.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–2</a></p>"},{"id":"l14-s2","title":"Plan space and algebraic equivalences","html":"<p>Optimisation has three concerns: the candidate plan space, the cost model and the search algorithm. Plan-space rules determine which relational expressions may replace one another without changing results.</p><p>Selections can be split and reordered. Projections can move earlier only if they preserve every attribute needed later. Inner joins can be reordered with their predicates, but an intermediate pair without a connecting condition is a Cartesian product. Outer joins and SQL NULL or duplicate semantics require additional care.</p><p>Changing a join tree is therefore more than swapping names in a diagram: predicates, output attributes and multiplicities must remain correct.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 3–4</a></p>"},{"id":"l14-s3","title":"Selection and projection pushdown","html":"<p>Selection pushdown applies a predicate as soon as its referenced attributes are available. It usually reduces the rows processed by later operators. Transitive equalities can expose additional legal joins, such as deriving R.A = T.C from R.A = S.B and S.B = T.C.</p><p>Projection pushdown removes attributes that no later predicate, join, grouping or output expression requires. Narrower tuples reduce intermediate storage and can change how much data fits in memory.</p><p>Avoiding unnecessary Cartesian products is another heuristic. These rules expose promising plans without requiring precise cost estimates, but their I/O effect depends on access methods, repeated scans and materialisation.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 5–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l14-s4","title":"Physical plan alternatives","html":"<p>A logical operator can have several physical implementations. Base-table access may use a heap scan or an applicable index. An equijoin may use block nested loops, index nested loops, sort-merge or hash join.</p><p>Each implementation has prerequisites and properties: index probes require a matching index, sort-merge requires suitable ordering or sorting, and hash join requires equality keys and memory management. General non-equality predicates can use nested loops.</p><p>The plan space combines access methods, join order and operator implementations. Subsequent lectures estimate the cost of these combinations and describe how to search them efficiently.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which columns must projection pushdown retain?","options":["Only the columns in the final SELECT list","Output columns and every column still needed by later operators","Only primary-key columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Join keys and filter columns remain necessary even when they are absent from the final SELECT list."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes the parser, rewriter, and cost-based optimizer; query blocks and the System R approach; logical equivalences and safe simplifications; join reordering, cross products, and transitive predicates; selection and projection pushdown; and physical implementation choices. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–7 (2×2; slides 1–28, numbering contains gaps)"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–2"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–5"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf#page=6","label":"PDF pages 6–7"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture14-query-optimisation-plan-space-2x2.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–7","topics":["Parser, rewriter, cost-based optimiser lifecycle","Query blocks and System R overview","Logical equivalences and safe simplifications","Join reorder, cross products, transitive predicates","Selection/projection pushdown heuristics","Physical implementation alternatives"],"limitations":["The official 2×2 slide-layout PDF was used because the one-slide-per-page version was unavailable during source review."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-15","lecture":15,"title":"Query optimisation: plan-space example","short":"Query optimisation: plan-space example","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"The Reserves/Sailors example from the source slides, with the same data sizes and cost assumptions.","goals":["Source query and selection pushdown","Join order and materialisation","Join algorithms and narrower tuples","Indexed plan and comparison"],"sections":[{"id":"l15-s1","title":"Source query and selection pushdown","html":"<p>The source uses Reserves with 1000 pages and 100 tuples/page, Sailors with 500 pages and 80 tuples/page, 100 equally likely boat IDs, 10 equally likely ratings and B = 5 join buffers. The query returns sname where R.sid = S.sid, R.bid = 100 and S.rating &gt; 5.</p><p>Page nested loops with Sailors outside costs 500 + 500·1000 = 500,500 I/Os. Filtering the outer input reduces its packed output to 250 page-equivalents, giving 500 + 250·1000 = 250,500.</p><p>Filtering the inner Reserves scan on the fly still reads all 1000 source pages on each rescan. It reduces output tuples but does not reduce that scan’s page reads. The estimates use the slides’ packed-stream accounting.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–13</a></p>"},{"id":"l15-s2","title":"Join order and materialisation","html":"<p>Move filtered Reserves to the outer input. The first scan costs 1000; its 1/100 fraction produces 10 page-equivalents. Ten Sailors rescans cost 10·500, giving 6000 I/Os.</p><p>Materialising filtered Sailors first costs a 500-page scan and 250 writes. Joining against those 250 temporary pages costs 1000 + 500 + 250 + 10·250 = 4250.</p><p>Reversing the orientation and materialising the 10-page Reserves result costs 500 + 1000 + 10 + 250·10 = 4010. A temporary relation incurs its write once and its read on every rescan; neither should be silently omitted.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 14–24</a></p>"},{"id":"l15-s3","title":"Join algorithms and narrower tuples","html":"<p>With B = 5, sorting the filtered 10-page Reserves stream requires two passes and the 250-page Sailors stream four. The source counts base scans once, initial sort writes, later sort reads/writes and a final merge: 1500 + 30 + 1750 + 260 = 3540 I/Os.</p><p>Block nested loops uses three outer frames. Streaming filtered Sailors against materialised Reserves costs 500 + 1000 + 10 + ⌈250/3⌉·10 = 2350.</p><p>Projecting filtered Reserves to its 4-byte sid reduces its 10 pages of 40-byte tuples to one page under the source packing assumptions. Keeping that outer input in memory permits one scan of each base relation: 1000 + 500 = 1500 I/Os.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 25–38</a></p>"},{"id":"l15-s4","title":"Indexed plan and comparison","html":"<p>The final plan assumes a clustered tree index on Reserves.bid, an unclustered tree index on Sailors.sid and index navigation already in memory. The bid predicate retrieves 1000 Reserves tuples on 10 data pages.</p><p>For each tuple, the sid index finds at most one Sailors record because sid is a key. At one data-page I/O per match, the estimate is 10 + 1000·1 = 1010 I/Os. Filtering rating afterwards does not remove the need to fetch that Sailors tuple.</p><p>The sequence is 500,500 → 250,500 → 6000 → 4250 → 4010 → 3540 → 2350 → 1500 → 1010. These are estimates for the source’s alternatives, not measurements or a proof that no other plan is cheaper.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=39\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 39–43</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Does filtering an inner heap from 600 pages to 12 output pages automatically reduce each scan to 12 I/Os?","options":["Yes, always","No, if each scan still reads the 600-page heap","It depends only on the number of SELECT columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Output size and the number of input pages that must be read are different quantities."},"source":{"summary":"The full official PDF has been reviewed. Coverage includes repeated plan refinement; selection pushdown inside and outside nested loops; join orientation; materializing filtered inner input; sort-merge pass accounting; block nested loops and projection width; and clustered outer access with an indexed inner join. The explanations and calculations are original teaching material rather than reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–43"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=14","label":"PDF pages 14–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=25","label":"PDF pages 25–38"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf#page=39","label":"PDF pages 39–43"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture15-query-optimisation-plan-space-example.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–43","topics":["Repeated plan refinement","Selection pushdown inside/outside nested loops","Join orientation","Materialising filtered inner input","Sort-merge pass accounting","Block nested loops, projection width","Clustered outer lookup and indexed inner join"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-16","lecture":16,"title":"Access methods","short":"Access methods","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Sequential scans, composite search keys, index-only scans and bitmap access.","goals":["Sequential scans and index selection","Composite keys and lexicographic ranges","Index-only, clustered and hash scans","Multi-index and bitmap scans"],"sections":[{"id":"l16-s1","title":"Sequential scans and index selection","html":"<p>An access path retrieves base-table tuples and applies applicable predicates. A sequential scan reads every table page and checks its tuples. For N pages its read cost is N even if only a small fraction of tuples qualifies.</p><p>Selectivity estimates output rows, not automatically source pages avoided. Reducing reads requires a suitable access path or metadata that can skip pages. A tree index supports equality and ranges; a hash index supports equality over its complete search key.</p><p>Choosing an index requires comparing its supported predicates, key uniqueness, clustering and matching-row count. Residual predicates still need to be checked after candidate records have been found.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l16-s2","title":"Composite keys and lexicographic ranges","html":"<p>A tree index on (A₁, A₂, …) orders keys lexicographically. A conventional contiguous lookup uses equalities on a leading prefix and optionally a range on the next attribute. Later conditions can be residual filters without narrowing the entire scan into one smaller interval.</p><p>In the source index (Age, Salary), Age = 55 AND Salary &gt; 200 forms a contiguous range. Age &gt; 31 AND Salary = 400 does not form one exact lexicographic interval: other salary values occur between matches.</p><p>An index on (Age, Salary) therefore differs from one on (Salary, Age). Conditions without a leading-key bound may require a broad index scan or a specialised access strategy rather than a direct prefix lookup.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7–15</a></p>"},{"id":"l16-s3","title":"Index-only, clustered and hash scans","html":"<p>An index-only scan can answer a query from attributes stored in index entries, without fetching the required values from heap records. The source illustrates grouping by dno using an index on dno, and MIN(salary) per department using (dno, salary). Real engines may additionally need visibility checks.</p><p>Clustered scans retrieve matching records with good data-page locality. Unclustered scans can fetch many pages; sorting candidate RIDs by page can reduce repeated or random access. Counting matching index entries is different from counting distinct data pages.</p><p>A composite hash index requires equality values for the complete hashed key. A hash index on (age, dept) matches both values together; age alone does not identify its bucket.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 16–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l16-s4","title":"Multi-index and bitmap scans","html":"<p>A multi-index path obtains candidate RID sets from several indexes. AND predicates generally intersect these sets; OR predicates can union sets when all relevant branches are represented. The engine fetches candidate records and checks residual predicates.</p><p>A bitmap represents candidate tuple positions or page locations compactly and can organise heap fetches by page. The PostgreSQL example separates Bitmap Index Scan, which constructs candidates, from Bitmap Heap Scan, which fetches records and rechecks conditions where required.</p><p>The source’s PostgreSQL exercise uses a Learn-provided dataset and EXPLAIN/EXPLAIN ANALYZE. Its restricted worksheet is not reproduced here. Access-path choice depends on table size, selectivity, available indexes and estimated cost.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–23</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which predicate forms a contiguous range in an index on (A,B)?","options":["A=7 AND B>20","B=20 with any value of A","A>7 AND B=20 with no residual check needed at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An equality prefix on A followed by a range on B forms one contiguous interval in the index."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Sequential scan and selectivity; Index eligibility and key ordering; Composite B+ tree prefix/range rule; Index-only, clustered, unclustered scans; Hash composite equality; Multi-index RID intersection/union; PostgreSQL EXPLAIN practical scope. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–23"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–7"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pages 8–16"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf#page=17","label":"PDF pages 17–23"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture16-access-methods0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–23","topics":["Sequential scan and selectivity","Index eligibility and key ordering","Composite B+ tree prefix/range rule","Index-only, clustered, unclustered scans","Hash composite equality","Multi-index RID intersection/union","PostgreSQL EXPLAIN practical scope"],"limitations":["The Learn practical and dataset require separate access and were not verified."]},"lab":"index"},{"id":"lecture-17","lecture":17,"title":"Query optimisation: cost estimation","short":"Query optimisation: cost estimation","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Catalog statistics, selectivity formulas, join cardinality and histograms.","goals":["Cost and cardinality statistics","Predicate selectivity","Join cardinality","Equi-width and equi-depth histograms"],"sections":[{"id":"l17-s1","title":"Cost and cardinality statistics","html":"<p>A plan’s cost depends on the cost and output size of every operator. An intermediate result becomes the next operator’s input, so cardinality-estimation errors can change subsequent join and memory decisions.</p><p>Catalog statistics include NTuples, NPages, minimum/maximum values, distinct-value counts NKeys and index height. They are refreshed periodically, so estimates can be approximate or stale. The source’s System R model combines page I/Os and a weighted tuple-processing term.</p><p>Selectivity is output cardinality divided by input cardinality, between zero and one. A “highly selective” predicate in ordinary usage usually has a small numeric selectivity. Estimated output pages additionally depend on tuple width and packing.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–5</a></p>"},{"id":"l17-s2","title":"Predicate selectivity","html":"<p>Under uniformity, equality to an existing value of A has estimated selectivity 1/V(A), where V(A) is its distinct count. For integer values uniformly covering [L,H], A &gt; a has selectivity approximately (H − a)/(H − L + 1), clamped to the valid range.</p><p>For independent predicates, sel(P AND Q) ≈ sel(P)·sel(Q), and sel(P OR Q) ≈ sel(P) + sel(Q) − sel(P)·sel(Q). The general union formula subtracts the actual intersection; independence supplies the product approximation.</p><p>sel(NOT P) = 1 − sel(P) assumes two-valued predicates over the population being estimated. SQL NULL/UNKNOWN and correlated attributes require additional treatment. These formulas are modelling assumptions, not identities for arbitrary real data.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 6–9</a></p>"},{"id":"l17-s3","title":"Join cardinality","html":"<p>For a non-null foreign key in S referencing a unique key in R, each S tuple has exactly one R match, so |R ⋈ S| = |S|. Nullable or unenforced foreign keys can reduce the inner-join result.</p><p>For a general equijoin on A, a common estimate is <code>|R|·|S| / max(V(R.A), V(S.A))</code>. It assumes uniform frequencies and containment of the smaller distinct-value domain in the larger one. Duplicate frequencies and partial domain overlap can violate the estimate.</p><p>Without adequate statistics, optimisers use defaults. Uniform estimates can be poor on skewed data; histograms retain more distribution information to improve estimates.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 10–14</a></p>"},{"id":"l17-s4","title":"Equi-width and equi-depth histograms","html":"<p>A histogram divides an attribute’s domain into intervals and stores counts and boundaries. Equi-width buckets have equal value-range widths; equi-depth buckets contain approximately equal tuple counts and therefore use narrower ranges in dense regions.</p><p>Equality estimation distributes a bucket’s frequency across its represented values or distinct-value count. Range estimation sums full covered buckets and estimates the covered fraction of boundary buckets. Uniformity is still assumed inside each bucket.</p><p>More buckets increase resolution and catalog space. Histograms address single-attribute skew but do not by themselves establish independence between different attributes. The source’s plots compare how the two bucket schemes allocate resolution.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 15–26</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"An equi-width bucket contains 4 values and 60 rows. What is the estimated equality count for one value?","options":["4","15","60"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Assuming uniformity within the bucket gives 60/4=15. This is an estimate based on equal frequencies, not the actual count if the data is skewed."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Operator costs and propagated cardinalities; Catalog statistics and refresh; Uniformity and independence; Equality/range/NOT/AND/OR selectivity; Key–foreign key and general equijoin size; Missing statistics defaults; Equi-width/equi-depth histogram equality/range estimates. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–12"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=13","label":"PDF pages 13–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–26"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture17-query-optimisation-costing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–26","topics":["Operator costs and propagated cardinalities","Catalog statistics and refresh","Uniformity and independence","Equality/range/NOT/AND/OR selectivity","Key–foreign key and general equijoin size","Missing statistics defaults","Equi-width/equi-depth histogram equality/range estimates"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-18","lecture":18,"title":"Query optimisation: plan search","short":"Query optimisation: plan search","group":"05 · Query optimisation","time":25,"lead":"Single-table access, join-tree enumeration, dynamic programming and interesting orders.","goals":["Single-table plan selection","Join trees and restricted search","Dynamic programming","Interesting orders and final plan cost"],"sections":[{"id":"l18-s1","title":"Single-table plan selection","html":"<p>Plan search enumerates candidates, estimates each cost and chooses the cheapest candidate considered. For one table, compare applicable indexes with a sequential scan, including predicate evaluation and required duplicate elimination or ordering.</p><p>The source example has 40,000 Sailors tuples, 500 data pages, ten rating values and a 50-page index. With uniform rating = 8, a clustered index estimate is (50 + 500)/10 = 55 I/Os; an unclustered estimate is (50 + 40,000)/10 = 4005. A sequential scan costs 500.</p><p>These approximate formulas expose why the same predicate can favour an index in one layout and a scan in another. Index navigation and caching details can refine the estimate.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–7</a></p>"},{"id":"l18-s2","title":"Join trees and restricted search","html":"<p>Join order, physical join method and base-table access paths multiply the number of alternatives. The number of possible trees grows rapidly even before physical implementations are considered.</p><p>System R restricts attention primarily to left-deep trees: each join’s right input is a base relation, and its left input can be an earlier join result. This supports index nested loops and often pipelines well, but a blocking physical operator can still interrupt a pipeline.</p><p>Avoiding Cartesian products further reduces candidates where connected joins are possible. Modern optimisers may consider bushy or other tree shapes; restricting the space trades optimisation time against missed alternatives.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 8–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l18-s3","title":"Dynamic programming","html":"<p>Dynamic programming reuses plans for relation subsets. Pass 1 chooses base-table access plans. Pass 2 combines pairs; later passes extend a retained subset plan by joining a relation not yet included.</p><p>For a candidate extension, estimate the complete accumulated cost: the retained subplan, access to the added relation and the chosen join operation, without double-counting reads already included in those components. Estimate result cardinality for later extensions.</p><p>The principle of optimality applies to states whose relevant output properties are represented. Retaining only one subplan per subset can lose the best complete plan if a more expensive subplan provides useful ordering.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l18-s4","title":"Interesting orders and final plan cost","html":"<p>An interesting order may reduce later sorting for a join, GROUP BY, DISTINCT or ORDER BY. The optimiser retains the cheapest unordered plan plus the cheapest plan for each useful output order for a relation subset.</p><p>The Sailors/Reserves/Boats example retains alternatives ordered by sid or bid when those orders can benefit later operations. Plans are compared within the same required-property state rather than discarding every plan that is not immediately cheapest.</p><p>After all joins, add remaining grouping, aggregation, duplicate elimination and sorting costs. The selected plan is the cheapest within the searched space under the estimated statistics and cost model.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–24</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Why retain a more expensive subplan that already produces sorted output?","options":["Because sorted output is always better","Because it may avoid an expensive sort later","Because subplan cost does not matter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An interesting order can reduce the total plan cost by avoiding a later sort, even if that subplan costs more."},"source":{"summary":"The complete official PDF was read. Topics covered: Single-table access enumeration and cost; Join-tree combinatorial growth; Left-deep versus bushy/right-deep; Cross-product pruning; Dynamic programming and optimal substructure; Interesting orders and property-aware retention; Final grouping/sorting cost. The explanations and calculations above are original teaching material, not reproductions of the slides.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=1","label":"Official PDF, pages 1–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–7"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=8","label":"PDF pages 8–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–24"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture18-query-optimisation-searching.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–24","topics":["Single-table access enumeration and cost","Join-tree combinatorial growth","Left-deep versus bushy/right-deep","Cross-product pruning","Dynamic programming and optimal substructure","Interesting orders and property-aware retention","Final grouping/sorting cost"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"join-cost"},{"id":"lecture-19","lecture":19,"title":"Transactions","short":"Transactions","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"Transaction semantics, ACID, serial schedules, conflicts and serializability.","goals":["Transaction model and concurrent execution","ACID and the source interleaving example","Schedules and conflicting operations"],"sections":[{"id":"l19-s1","title":"Transaction model and concurrent execution","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l19-s1\"></span><p>A transaction groups database operations into one unit beginning with BEGIN and ending in COMMIT or ABORT/ROLLBACK. A commit accepts its changes; an abort removes its effects. The abstract model represents access to named objects using Rᵢ(A) and Wᵢ(A).</p><p>The source transfers £100 between checking and savings by debiting one account and crediting the other. The intermediate state is not the completed transaction result. External effects outside the database are not automatically rolled back by the DBMS.</p><p>Concurrent execution overlaps work to improve resource utilisation, throughput and response time. Correctness requires controlling the resulting interleaving of read and write operations.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–11</a></p>"},{"id":"l19-s2","title":"ACID and the source interleaving example","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l19-s2\"></span><p>Atomicity means all-or-nothing effects. Consistency requires transactions and enforced constraints to preserve valid database states. Isolation constrains interference between concurrent transactions. Durability preserves committed effects through the failures covered by the recovery model.</p><p>Logging and shadow paging support atomicity and durability; pessimistic or optimistic concurrency control supports isolation. Application logic remains responsible for expressing a correct transaction.</p><p>The source starts A = B = 1000, transfers 100 in T1 and applies 6% interest in T2. The two serial orders give (954,1166) or (960,1160), both totalling 2120. An invalid interleaving gives (954,1160), totalling 2114. Preserving each transaction’s internal order alone is insufficient.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 12–23</a></p>"},{"id":"l19-s3","title":"Schedules and conflicting operations","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l19-s3\"></span><p>A schedule contains operations from several transactions while preserving each transaction’s own operation order. A serial schedule runs transactions without interleaving. A serializable schedule has an effect equivalent to an appropriate serial execution under the chosen equivalence notion.</p><p>Two operations conflict when they belong to different transactions, access the same object and at least one writes. Read–read pairs do not conflict. Read–write, write–read and write–write orders can affect values observed or retained.</p><p>The source relates these conflicts to unrepeatable reads, dirty reads and overwriting uncommitted data. Commit/abort ordering also matters for recovery; serializability alone does not specify every failure-handling property.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 24–31</a></p>"},{"id":"l19-s4","title":"Conflict serializability and precedence graphs","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l19-s4\"></span><p>Two schedules are conflict equivalent if they contain the same transaction operations and preserve the order of every conflicting pair. A schedule is conflict serializable when it is conflict equivalent to a serial schedule.</p><p>Construct a graph with one vertex per transaction. Add Tᵢ → Tⱼ whenever an operation of Tᵢ precedes a conflicting operation of Tⱼ on the same object. Include every such pair, not only neighbouring operations.</p><p>The schedule is conflict serializable exactly when the graph is acyclic. A topological order gives an equivalent serial order. A cycle rejects conflict serializability even if one particular numerical execution happens to produce an acceptable result.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 32–42</a></p>"},{"id":"l19-s5","title":"View serializability and durability","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l19-s5\"></span><p>View equivalence preserves which reads obtain initial values, which transaction each other read obtains its value from, and which transaction performs the final write of each object. A schedule is view serializable if it is view equivalent to a serial schedule.</p><p>Every conflict-serializable schedule is view serializable, but blind writes allow additional view-serializable schedules. Testing the broader property is harder; the course uses conflict serializability as the practical basis for locking protocols.</p><p>Durability is a separate requirement: acknowledged commits must survive a crash. Logging or shadow paging supplies the persistent information needed to preserve committed changes and remove aborted work.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 43–48</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Is R1(A), W2(A), W1(B), R2(B) conflict-serializable?","options":["Yes, in the order T1 then T2","Yes, in the order T2 then T1","No, because the graph has a cycle","There is insufficient information because two objects are involved"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both R1(A) before W2(A) and W1(B) before R2(B) create T1 → T2. There is no edge in the reverse direction."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 19 introduces transactions, ACID, execution schedules, conflicts, and two serializability criteria. A dependency graph checks conflict serializability, while logging or shadow paging supports atomicity and durability.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–42"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=29","label":"PDF page 29 · slide 32 (dirty read); pages 28–30 (anomalies)"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf#page=43","label":"PDF pages 43–48"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture19-transactions.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–48","topics":["ACID","transaction abstraction","schedules","RW/WR/WW anomalies","conflict equivalence","precedence graph","view equivalence","durability"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"transactions"},{"id":"lecture-20","lecture":20,"title":"Locking","short":"Locking","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"Lock compatibility, two-phase locking, strict locking and deadlock handling.","goals":["Lock modes and compatibility","Two-phase locking","Strict locking and the source schedule"],"sections":[{"id":"l20-s1","title":"Lock modes and compatibility","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l20-s1\"></span><p>A lock manager grants access before operations execute. Shared (S) locks permit reads; exclusive (X) locks protect writes. Several transactions may hold S on the same object, but X conflicts with both S and X held by another transaction.</p><p>An incompatible request waits or triggers an abort according to the concurrency-control policy. The lock table records holders and waiting requests. Upgrading S to X must also satisfy compatibility.</p><p>Acquiring a lock for each operation is insufficient for serializability if a transaction releases a lock and later reacquires locks freely. A protocol must constrain the lifetime of those locks.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l20-s2","title":"Two-phase locking","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l20-s2\"></span><p>Two-phase locking (2PL) separates a transaction into a growing phase, in which it acquires or upgrades locks, and a shrinking phase, in which it releases locks. After its first release it cannot acquire a new lock or upgrade one.</p><p>2PL guarantees conflict serializability for the lock-protected objects, but admits only a subset of conflict-serializable schedules. It does not require knowing every future operation in advance.</p><p>Basic 2PL can release write locks before commit and allow another transaction to read uncommitted data, causing cascading aborts. It can also deadlock. These are distinct limitations addressed by stricter lock retention and deadlock handling.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l20-s3","title":"Strict locking and the source schedule","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l20-s3\"></span><p>The source’s strict-2PL presentation retains all locks until transaction completion. Terminology varies: retaining X locks to the end is often called strict 2PL, while retaining both S and X locks is also called rigorous 2PL.</p><p>Retaining write locks prevents other transactions from reading or overwriting uncommitted changes. On abort, restoring old values does not invalidate committed readers of those uncommitted values because such readers were blocked.</p><p>The source compares a transfer of 100 from A to B with a transaction summing both balances. Releasing and reacquiring locks without 2PL permits the incorrect total 1900. The controlled schedules return the consistent total 2000.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–17</a></p>"},{"id":"l20-s4","title":"Deadlock detection","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l20-s4\"></span><p>A deadlock occurs when transactions form a cycle of waiting for each other’s locks. It is represented by a waits-for graph: Tᵢ → Tⱼ means Tᵢ is waiting for a lock held by Tⱼ. This differs from the precedence graph used to test schedule serializability.</p><p>The DBMS periodically searches for cycles. To break one it aborts a victim and releases its locks. Victim choice can consider age, work performed, held locks and previous restarts; fairness policies reduce starvation.</p><p>Detection frequency trades checking overhead against waiting time. Conservative 2PL avoids deadlocks by acquiring all required locks before execution, at the cost of knowing them in advance and potentially reducing concurrency.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 18–22</a></p>"},{"id":"l20-s5","title":"Wait-die and wound-wait","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l20-s5\"></span><p>Assign each transaction a timestamp; older transactions have higher priority. In <strong>wait-die</strong>, an older requester may wait for a younger holder; a younger requester aborts when the holder is older.</p><p>In <strong>wound-wait</strong>, an older requester aborts a younger holder; a younger requester waits for an older holder. Only one age direction is allowed for waits in each scheme, so a waiting cycle cannot form.</p><p>Restarted transactions retain their original timestamp to avoid repeatedly losing priority. The rules refer specifically to requester and holder; reversing those roles reverses the decision.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 23–28</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"T_old requests a lock held by T_new. What does wound-wait do?","options":["T_old aborts","T_new aborts and releases its locks","T_old always waits","Both transactions receive the X-lock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under wound-wait, a transaction with a smaller timestamp can force a younger transaction holding the requested lock to abort."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 20 covers shared and exclusive locks, 2PL, strict 2PL, deadlock detection, and the wait-die and wound-wait prevention rules.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–22"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=23","label":"PDF pages 23–27"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf#page=7","label":"PDF pages 7–28"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture20-locking.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–28","topics":["lock compatibility","growing/shrinking phases","strict 2PL","cascading aborts","waits-for graph","victim selection","wait-die","wound-wait"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"transactions"},{"id":"lecture-21","lecture":21,"title":"Recovery","short":"Recovery","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"Failure models, STEAL/FORCE policies, write-ahead logging and ARIES phases.","goals":["Failures and recovery responsibilities","STEAL and FORCE policies","UNDO, REDO and logging"],"sections":[{"id":"l21-s1","title":"Failures and recovery responsibilities","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l21-s1\"></span><p>Recovery preserves atomicity and durability when transactions abort or the system crashes. It includes actions during normal execution that create recovery information and actions after restart that use it.</p><p>The source distinguishes transaction failures, system failures and media failures. Its fail-stop crash model assumes persistent storage survives a system crash. Destroyed storage requires an independent backup or replica and suitable restoration procedures.</p><p>Updates occur in volatile buffers before data pages reach persistent storage. Recovery must distinguish committed work whose pages may be missing from disk from uncommitted work whose pages may already have reached disk.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–6</a></p>"},{"id":"l21-s2","title":"STEAL and FORCE policies","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l21-s2\"></span><p>STEAL permits flushing a page containing uncommitted updates; NO-STEAL forbids that. FORCE requires a transaction’s changed data pages to be persistent at commit; NO-FORCE does not.</p><p>NO-STEAL/FORCE avoids uncommitted disk updates and missing committed pages under its idealised completed-write assumptions. It imposes memory pressure and expensive commit-time page writes. A crash during multi-page installation still requires a mechanism for atomic completion.</p><p>The policy names describe permission and obligation, not individual events: STEAL does not mean every dirty page is flushed early, and NO-FORCE does not prohibit pages from being written before commit.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 7–15</a></p>"},{"id":"l21-s3","title":"UNDO, REDO and logging","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l21-s3\"></span><p>STEAL creates a need for UNDO because an aborted or unfinished transaction may have modified persistent data. NO-FORCE creates a need for REDO because a committed transaction’s updates may exist only in lost memory.</p><p>STEAL/NO-FORCE combines flexible buffer replacement with inexpensive commit-time data handling, but requires both recovery capabilities. A log stores enough information to reverse or reapply updates.</p><p>Log writes are typically sequential and can pack information for several updates into one page. The log and data pages are different persistent objects; a durable commit does not require every corresponding data page to have been flushed.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 16–18</a></p>"},{"id":"l21-s4","title":"Write-ahead logging rules","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l21-s4\"></span><p>Before an updated data page is written to persistent storage, the relevant update log records must already be persistent. This ordering preserves the information needed to undo an uncommitted page write or redo a committed update.</p><p>Before a commit is acknowledged, the transaction’s log records through its commit record must be durable. Appending a record to an in-memory log buffer is not the same as flushing it to persistent storage.</p><p>The source changes A from 1 to 8 and B from 5 to 9. After the update and commit records are flushed, a crash can be recovered even if the data pages still contain old values. WAL orders log and data persistence; it does not require immediate data-page writes.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 19–24</a></p>"},{"id":"l21-s5","title":"ARIES analysis, redo and undo","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l21-s5\"></span><p>ARIES supports recovery with WAL and STEAL/NO-FORCE. Analysis starts from a checkpoint and reconstructs transaction and dirty-page information needed to continue recovery.</p><p>Redo repeats the relevant history, including logged updates of transactions that may later be undone. It does not simply replay committed transactions. Page/log sequence information determines whether a logged action already appears on disk.</p><p>Undo reverses the remaining effects of loser transactions that did not complete successfully. The source introduces these three phases; a complete ARIES implementation additionally needs detailed log-record, checkpoint and compensation-record machinery.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 25–27</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What does STEAL + NO-FORCE typically require?","options":["REDO only","UNDO only","Both UNDO and REDO","No log is needed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"STEAL can put uncommitted data on disk, while NO-FORCE can leave committed data only in RAM."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 21 connects buffer policies to recovery, explains WAL, and introduces the three ARIES phases: analysis, redo, and undo.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=2","label":"PDF pages 2–17"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–24"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=25","label":"PDF pages 25–27"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–5"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture21-recovery.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–27","topics":["failure types","buffer policies","UNDO/REDO","logging","WAL ordering","commit durability","ARIES phases"],"limitations":["The lecture introduces ARIES at a high level; it is not a complete implementation specification."]},"lab":"recovery"},{"id":"lecture-22","lecture":22,"title":"Distributed transactions","short":"Distributed transactions","group":"06 · Transactions","time":25,"lead":"Distributed locking, global deadlocks, two-phase commit and recovery.","goals":["Partitioned data and distributed locking","Global deadlocks and commitment","Two-phase commit protocol"],"sections":[{"id":"l22-s1","title":"Partitioned data and distributed locking","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l22-s1\"></span><p>A distributed transaction accesses data on multiple nodes while presenting one transaction to the application. This lecture assumes shared-nothing nodes with partitioned, nonreplicated data. A coordinator organises each transaction’s work.</p><p>Locks can be partitioned with the data so each node manages locks for its local objects. Coarser objects spanning nodes can have a designated home lock manager or a central manager.</p><p>Local lock correctness does not settle global deadlock or atomic commitment. Every participating node must agree on whether the transaction commits, including when nodes or messages fail.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l22-s2","title":"Global deadlocks and commitment","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l22-s2\"></span><p>A waits-for cycle may span nodes even when every local graph is acyclic. Combining local dependencies can reveal a global cycle; aborting an appropriate victim breaks it. Distributed detection must account for information that can be delayed.</p><p>Commit has a different requirement: every participant must be ready. A local constraint violation or abort vote prevents a global commit. Missing messages and crashed nodes make collecting and disseminating the decision difficult.</p><p>Timeouts indicate that progress or communication is missing; they do not by themselves prove what a remote node has durably decided. This distinction drives the logging requirements of two-phase commit.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–21</a></p>"},{"id":"l22-s3","title":"Two-phase commit protocol","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l22-s3\"></span><p>In phase 1, the coordinator sends PREPARE and gathers YES/NO votes. A participant voting YES promises it can commit later and must not independently abandon that prepared transaction. Commit requires YES from all participants.</p><p>In phase 2, the coordinator sends the global COMMIT or ABORT decision. Participants apply that decision and acknowledge it. Two-phase commit (2PC) decides transaction outcome; two-phase locking (2PL) controls conflicting access. They solve different problems.</p><p>If the coordinator cannot obtain all required votes before deciding, it can abort. Once a commit decision is durable, retrying communication must preserve that decision.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 22–30</a></p>"},{"id":"l22-s4","title":"Persistent protocol state","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l22-s4\"></span><p>Before voting YES, a participant flushes its PREPARE record and the recovery information needed to fulfil that promise. Before announcing COMMIT, the coordinator flushes the decision and records the participant identities needed for recovery.</p><p>Participants log and persist the received decision before acknowledging as required by the presented protocol. The coordinator records completion after collecting acknowledgements. The source marks forced log records with an asterisk to show message-order dependencies.</p><p>These records allow a restarted process to distinguish an undecided transaction from a decided one. A message sent without the required preceding flush could otherwise report a decision that disappears after a crash.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 31–48</a></p>"},{"id":"l22-s5","title":"Recovery, strict locking and blocking","html":"<span id=\"bilingual-check-l22-s5\"></span><p>A recovered coordinator with a durable commit decision resumes phase 2 if acknowledgements are incomplete. A prepared participant without a final decision must inquire and wait for reliable decision information. The source’s presumed-abort rules depend on the process’s role and log state.</p><p>Strict locking keeps uncertain transactions’ locks until their outcome is resolved. The source also orders messages per sender/receiver/transaction so a receiver does not process a later operation before missing earlier work.</p><p>2PC can block when a prepared participant cannot learn the coordinator’s decision. A timeout cannot safely choose between commit and abort in that state. Replicated commit protocols address additional failure cases; their details are outside this lecture.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=49\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 49–57</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A participant has only a durable PREPARE record and loses contact with the coordinator. What is safe?","options":["Commit independently because it voted YES","Abort immediately when a timeout expires","Seek the decision; it may have to wait","Delete the log and start over"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A YES vote is a promise to follow the common decision. The coordinator may already have committed elsewhere."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 22 studies partitioned, nonreplicated data, distributed locking, global deadlock, two-phase commit, and the coordination of logging with recovery. Two-phase commit can block progress when the coordinator is unreachable.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=3","label":"PDF pages 3–20"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=21","label":"PDF pages 21–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=32","label":"PDF pages 32–54"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=47","label":"PDF pages 47–57"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf#page=55","label":"PDF pages 55–57"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture22-distributed-transactions.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–57","topics":["partitioned locks","global deadlock","2PC unanimity","prepare logging","decision logging","recovery cases","strict 2PL integration","blocking"],"limitations":["The lecture assumes partitioning without replication. Consensus-based improvements are mentioned but not developed in detail."]},"lab":"distributed"},{"id":"lecture-23","lecture":23,"title":"Conjunctive queries: syntax and semantics","short":"Conjunctive queries: syntax and semantics","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"Relational calculus, CQ rules, substitutions, homomorphisms and query answers.","goals":["Algebra and declarative query languages","CQ syntax: head and body","The Flight/Airport source query"],"sections":[{"id":"l23-s1","title":"Algebra and declarative query languages","html":"<p>Relational algebra gives an operational expression using selection, projection and joins. Relational calculus describes output values using logical formulas. The DBMS translates a declarative query into a plan that can be optimised and executed.</p><p>The source database has Flight(origin, destination, airline) and Airport(code, city). Projection retrieves airlines; selection and projection retrieve London airport codes; joining two renamed Airport references with Flight identifies direct flights between specified cities.</p><p>Conjunctive queries capture the selection/projection/join fragment under set semantics. Their logical form uses conjunction and existential quantification, without negation or disjunction in the basic language.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–14</a></p>"},{"id":"l23-s2","title":"CQ syntax: head and body","html":"<p>Write a CQ as <code>Q(x) :- R₁(v₁), …, Rₘ(vₘ)</code>. The head lists distinguished/output variables. The body is a conjunction of relational atoms. Variables used only in the body are existentially quantified.</p><p>An atom names a relation and gives a term for each attribute position. Terms can be variables or constants. Reusing a variable requires the same value in every occurrence, so repeated variables express equality constraints.</p><p>Output variables must occur in the body for the range-restricted queries considered here. A Boolean CQ has no output variables and asks whether any assignment satisfies the body. Constants retain their specified values.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 15–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l23-s3","title":"The Flight/Airport source query","html":"<p>The source expresses direct airlines from London to Glasgow as <code>Q(z) :- Airport(x,London), Airport(y,Glasgow), Flight(x,y,z)</code>. London and Glasgow are constants; x and y are existential variables; z is the output variable.</p><p>An answer needs an origin airport in London, a destination airport in Glasgow and a Flight tuple connecting those same codes. Finding one atom independently is insufficient: shared variables must agree across all atoms.</p><p>The listed data contains Airport(LGW,London), Airport(GLA,Glasgow) and Flight(LGW,GLA,U2). They support the output U2. The result is a set, so several supporting flights with the same airline would not duplicate that airline in the answer.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–25</a></p>"},{"id":"l23-s4","title":"Substitutions and homomorphisms","html":"<p>A substitution maps terms to terms. A homomorphism h from atom set A to atom set B fixes every constant and maps every atom of A into an atom present in B. The same mapping is used for every occurrence of a variable.</p><p>To check a proposed mapping, replace every argument in each source atom and test membership of the resulting atom in the target. Relation names and argument positions are preserved.</p><p>Different source variables may map to the same target value: a homomorphism need not be injective. It also need not cover every target atom. These properties distinguish pattern matching from a one-to-one correspondence between entire databases.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 26–30</a></p>"},{"id":"l23-s5","title":"Semantics of a CQ answer","html":"<p>A match of Q in database D is a homomorphism from Q’s body to D. For each match h, output the head tuple h(x). Thus <code>Q(D) = {h(x) | h(body) ⊆ D}</code>, with constants fixed.</p><p>For the source flight query, h(x) = LGW, h(y) = GLA and h(z) = U2 satisfies all three atoms, giving the output tuple (U2). Values for existential variables are witnesses rather than additional output columns.</p><p>Several matches can yield the same output tuple, which appears once under set semantics. Evaluating the query means finding whether matches exist and collecting their head values.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 31–33</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"In Q(x) :- R(x,y), S(y), what is y?","options":["A constant","An output variable","An existentially quantified variable shared by the two atoms","Two independent variables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The variable y does not appear in the head, but every occurrence of y must receive the same value within an assignment."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 23 connects relational algebra and calculus with conjunctive queries. It defines head and body syntax, homomorphisms, and result semantics in terms of matches.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–25"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=26","label":"PDF pages 26–33"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pages 19–33"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture23-cqs-syntax-and-semantics.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–33","topics":["RA recap","relational calculus","Codd theorem context","CQ syntax","distinguished variables","homomorphism","constants","set semantics"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-24","lecture":24,"title":"Conjunctive queries: evaluation and static analysis","short":"Conjunctive queries: evaluation and static analysis","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"Evaluation complexity, canonical databases, containment, equivalence and minimisation.","goals":["Evaluation and data complexity","Why combined evaluation is NP-complete","Static questions and canonical databases"],"sections":[{"id":"l24-s1","title":"Evaluation and data complexity","html":"<p>The decision version of CQ evaluation asks whether a supplied tuple belongs to Q(D). Combined complexity treats both query and database as input. Data complexity fixes the query and varies the database.</p><p>CQ evaluation is NP-complete in combined complexity and polynomial-time in data complexity. For a fixed query with v variables, enumerating assignments has an exponent depending on that fixed v; when the query varies, that exponent is no longer constant.</p><p>The decision formulation separates match existence from the possible size of the complete result. A large answer still takes time to write, even when the corresponding membership or Boolean problem is tractable.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–12</a></p>"},{"id":"l24-s2","title":"Why combined evaluation is NP-complete","html":"<p>NP membership follows by guessing an assignment to query variables and verifying every instantiated atom against D, including the required output tuple. The certificate has polynomial size in the input.</p><p>For hardness, the source relates graph three-colourability to a homomorphism into the three-colour complete graph K₃. Query variables represent graph vertices and atoms enforce edges. A match assigns colours while respecting every adjacency constraint.</p><p>This proof concerns arbitrary input queries. It does not imply that every particular CQ or structurally restricted CQ is difficult. Later results exploit query structure to obtain tractable classes.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 13–18</a></p>"},{"id":"l24-s3","title":"Static questions and canonical databases","html":"<p>Satisfiability asks whether some database gives a nonempty answer. Containment Q₁ ⊆ Q₂ asks whether Q₁(D) ⊆ Q₂(D) for every D. Equivalence requires both containment directions.</p><p>The canonical database D[Q] replaces each query variable with a fresh distinct constant, preserving repeated occurrences and existing constants. Its facts instantiate the query body. The resulting head tuple is an answer to Q on that database.</p><p>Consequently positive CQs in this language are satisfiable. Extra constraints, negation or contradictory built-in comparisons would change the assumptions; the satisfiability statement is for the basic language presented here.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 19–25</a></p>"},{"id":"l24-s4","title":"Containment through homomorphisms","html":"<p>A query homomorphism maps body atoms and preserves the ordered head tuple. The containment theorem reverses the apparent direction: <code>Q₁ ⊆ Q₂ iff there is a query homomorphism Q₂ → Q₁</code>.</p><p>For the forward proof, evaluate Q₂ on the canonical database of Q₁: containment forces Q₁’s canonical head tuple to appear in Q₂’s result. This match induces the reverse query mapping. For the other direction, compose Q₂ → Q₁ with any match of Q₁ in a database.</p><p>Equivalence is checked by mappings both ways; containment and equivalence are NP-complete in general. A comparison on one database cannot establish a property quantified over every database.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 26–31</a></p>"},{"id":"l24-s5","title":"CQ minimisation and cores","html":"<p>Minimisation removes redundant atoms while preserving query equivalence and head variables. To remove atom α, keep the head range-restricted and check for a query homomorphism from the current query into the query without α. The reverse mapping is supplied by inclusion of the remaining body.</p><p>Repeat until no atom can be removed. In the source example, R(x,y) is redundant given R(x,b), and R(u,v) is redundant given R(u,c), using y ↦ b and v ↦ c. Mapping the distinguished variable x to constant a is not allowed when it changes the head.</p><p>Minimal equivalent CQs are unique up to variable renaming; this minimal structure is the query’s core. Reducing atom count can remove unnecessary joins.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 32–37</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"To prove Q1 ⊆ Q2 using the homomorphism theorem, which mapping should you seek?","options":["Q1 → Q2","Q2 → Q1","Database → Q1","Only compare the number of atoms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The mapping goes against the direction of containment and must preserve the head and the constants."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 24 studies the complexity of conjunctive-query evaluation, canonical databases, satisfiability, containment and equivalence through homomorphisms, and minimization to a core.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=10","label":"PDF pages 10–18"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=19","label":"PDF pages 19–29"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=23","label":"PDF pages 23–31"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf#page=32","label":"PDF pages 32–37"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture24-cqs-evaluation-and-static-analysis_0.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–37","topics":["combined complexity","data complexity","NP witness","3-colorability reduction","canonical database","satisfiability","homomorphism theorem","containment/equivalence","minimization","core"],"limitations":["These theoretical results apply to conjunctive queries under set semantics. They do not automatically apply to SQL bags, NULL values, or additional constraints."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-25","lecture":25,"title":"Conjunctive queries: fast evaluation","short":"Conjunctive queries: fast evaluation","group":"07 · Conjunctive queries","time":25,"lead":"Query graphs and hypergraphs, acyclicity, GYO reduction and Yannakakis evaluation.","goals":["Structural restrictions on queries","Join trees and acyclicity","GYO reduction"],"sections":[{"id":"l25-s1","title":"Structural restrictions on queries","html":"<p>General CQ evaluation is NP-complete in combined complexity, but restrictions on query structure can make it tractable. The source distinguishes graph-based and hypergraph-based restrictions.</p><p>The query graph connects variables that occur together in an atom. The query hypergraph represents each atom by a hyperedge containing its variables. A high-arity atom is therefore represented directly rather than only through pairwise edges.</p><p>Bounded treewidth restricts the graph; bounded hypertree width restricts the hypergraph. Acyclic CQs form an important hypergraph-based class. A cycle in the ordinary graph does not by itself establish hypergraph cyclicity.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–5</a></p>"},{"id":"l25-s2","title":"Join trees and acyclicity","html":"<p>A join tree has nodes labelled by query hyperedges. Every hyperedge is represented. For each variable, all nodes whose labels contain that variable must form a connected subtree: the running-intersection property.</p><p>A hypergraph is acyclic if it admits such a tree. Connectivity for each individual variable ensures that consistency of values can be propagated through adjacent relation pairs.</p><p>The source contrasts a triangle of pairwise edges with a structure containing an additional encompassing hyperedge. The latter can have a join tree even though its ordinary graph contains a triangle. Acyclicity here is the hypergraph notion.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 6–13</a></p>"},{"id":"l25-s3","title":"GYO reduction","html":"<p>GYO repeatedly removes a vertex occurring in at most one hyperedge and removes an empty hyperedge or one contained in another. Apply the rules until no rule changes the hypergraph.</p><p>The hypergraph is acyclic exactly when this process reduces it to the empty hypergraph. A remaining irreducible structure witnesses failure of the test. Removal of contained edges is essential; deleting only low-degree vertices is insufficient.</p><p>The source gives a full reduction sequence and states that acyclicity testing and join-tree construction are tractable, with linear-time algorithms in the query size. The reduction is a structural test, independent of current database tuples.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 14–26</a></p>"},{"id":"l25-s4","title":"Boolean evaluation by semijoins","html":"<p>The presented Yannakakis algorithm evaluates an acyclic Boolean CQ. Construct a join tree and associate each node with the database relation for its atom. Process children before their parent.</p><p>For each child C of parent P, replace P by P ⋉ C on their shared variables, after C has already been reduced by its descendants. A semijoin keeps parent tuples with at least one matching child tuple; it adds no child columns and does not multiply parent tuples.</p><p>The running-intersection property ensures that the subtree constraints are represented by the surviving tuples at each step. After all children are processed, the root records which assignments have support throughout the tree.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 27–35</a></p>"},{"id":"l25-s5","title":"Decision result and output-size limits","html":"<p>For the Boolean problem, return YES if the reduced root relation is nonempty and NO otherwise. The source example ends with a surviving root tuple and therefore returns YES.</p><p>The source discusses efficient acyclic query evaluation in terms of database and query size. This does not mean that all tuples of a large non-Boolean answer can be written independently of its output size.</p><p>Full result enumeration generally adds a top-down reduction and join/enumeration work, with output-sensitive accounting. The slides’ bottom-up Boolean test should not be confused with a complete implementation that enumerates every projected answer.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=36\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 36–37</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"GYO reduction stops with the triangle {a,b}, {b,c}, {c,a}. What follows?","options":["It is acyclic because it has three edges","It is cyclic because reduction cannot eliminate the whole hypergraph","Every possible database must be tested","No query corresponds to this structure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No variable has degree at most 1, and no edge is contained in another. The nonempty structure left by GYO reduction establishes that the hypergraph is cyclic."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 25 introduces conjunctive-query graphs and hypergraphs, useful structural classes, join trees, GYO reduction, and Yannakakis’s algorithm for acyclic queries.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=4","label":"PDF pages 4–13"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=14","label":"PDF pages 14–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–36"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf#page=28","label":"PDF pages 28–37"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture25-cqs-fast-evaluation.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–37","topics":["graph/hypergraph","treewidth context","join tree","running intersection","GYO reduction","acyclicity","Yannakakis","semijoin","Boolean evaluation"],"limitations":["The stated time bound does not include enumerating an arbitrarily large output. This lesson focuses on the decision problem."]},"lab":"relational"},{"id":"lecture-26","lecture":26,"title":"Parallel query processing","short":"Parallel query processing","group":"08 · Distributed systems","time":25,"lead":"Shared-resource architectures, data placement, parallel operators and distributed join plans.","goals":["Architectures and levels of parallelism","Partitioning and replication","Parallel scans, hashing and sorting"],"sections":[{"id":"l26-s1","title":"Architectures and levels of parallelism","html":"<p>Shared-memory processors access a common address space; shared-disk nodes have private memory but common storage; shared-nothing nodes have private CPU, memory and storage and communicate by network. Each architecture changes coordination and bottlenecks.</p><p>Inter-query parallelism runs different queries concurrently. Intra-query parallelism divides one query: inter-operator parallelism overlaps different operators, while intra-operator parallelism divides one operator’s work across partitions.</p><p>Pipeline parallelism is constrained by blocking operators. Independent branches can run in parallel, and partition-parallel scans or joins can process disjoint work simultaneously. The logical query answer must remain unchanged.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–11</a></p>"},{"id":"l26-s2","title":"Partitioning and replication","html":"<p>Horizontal partitioning assigns whole tuples to disjoint partitions. Round-robin spreads tuples without key locality. Hash partitioning colocates equal partition keys. Range partitioning assigns key intervals and supports range-based pruning.</p><p>Replication stores additional copies of a partition or an entire table. It can improve availability and local reads but introduces update-propagation and consistency work. Replication and partitioning are independent design choices.</p><p>Data transparency hides placement from applications: the same logical query should return the same answer despite partitioning or replication. The optimiser still needs placement metadata to determine local work and communication.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 12–19</a></p>"},{"id":"l26-s3","title":"Parallel scans, hashing and sorting","html":"<p>A parallel scan reads local partitions and combines their results. A predicate on the partition key can prune irrelevant partitions; a lookup without usable placement information may need broadcasting.</p><p>Parallel hash join partitions both inputs by the same join-key function, then performs local joins. Matching keys must reach the same node. Parallel sorting assigns nonoverlapping key ranges to nodes and sorts locally; global ordering requires combining ranges in order.</p><p>Parallel sort-merge join likewise uses compatible ranges on both inputs. Data exchange is part of the physical plan, not an optional step that can be omitted from cost estimates.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 20–26</a></p>"},{"id":"l26-s4","title":"Four distributed join layouts","html":"<p>If one input is replicated, each node joins its disjoint local partition of the other input with that replica. If both inputs are compatibly partitioned on the join key, local joins require no repartitioning.</p><p>If placement is incompatible and one input is small, broadcast the small input to all join workers. If neither input is suitably placed or cheap to replicate, repartition both on the join key and join corresponding partitions.</p><p>Each plan must avoid duplicate result production when replicas are present. Its cost includes bytes moved, local join work, memory needs and collecting the output. The source illustrates these scenarios across successive node-placement figures.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 27–42</a></p>"},{"id":"l26-s5","title":"Network cost and skew","html":"<p>Predicate and projection pushdown, join ordering and access-method selection remain relevant in a distributed plan. They also reduce bytes sent before an exchange.</p><p>Parallel latency follows the critical path rather than the sum of all worker times. If workers finish in 500 ms and 300 ms, that stage completes after 500 ms before accounting for other dependent stages.</p><p>Skew can concentrate tuples or expensive keys on one worker, making extra workers ineffective. The optimiser must consider placement, network transfer, dependencies and the slowest partition alongside local I/O and CPU work.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 43–45</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Workers take 4, 5, 5, and 15 seconds in one parallel phase. What is the lower bound on the phase duration?","options":["7.25 seconds","4 seconds","15 seconds","29 seconds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The phase must wait for its slowest worker. Coordination and network overhead may increase the total duration further."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 26 compares resource-sharing architectures, levels of parallelism, partitioning and replication, parallel scans, sorts, and joins, and cost models that account for network traffic and skew.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–11"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=12","label":"PDF pages 12–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=20","label":"PDF pages 20–26"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=27","label":"PDF pages 27–42"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf#page=43","label":"PDF pages 43–45"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture26-parallel-query-processing.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–45","topics":["shared architectures","inter/intra-query parallelism","pipeline/bushy/partition parallelism","round-robin/hash/range","replication","parallel scan","parallel sort/hash join","broadcast/shuffle","network cost","skew"],"limitations":[]},"lab":"distributed"},{"id":"lecture-27","lecture":27,"title":"NoSQL systems","short":"NoSQL systems","group":"08 · Distributed systems","time":25,"lead":"Scaling through sharding and replication, CAP, BASE and NoSQL data models.","goals":["Motivation, sharding and replication","CAP theorem","BASE and key-value/column-family models"],"sections":[{"id":"l27-s1","title":"Motivation, sharding and replication","html":"<p>Large web workloads motivate horizontal scaling and simpler operations with less cross-node coordination. Sharding partitions data and work; replication creates copies that can serve reads or survive a node failure.</p><p>Sharding can make multi-partition reads and transactions expensive. Replication requires propagating writes and deciding what reads observe while copies differ. More machines do not remove these coordination requirements.</p><p>NoSQL describes a family of systems, not one fixed consistency or transaction model. Flexible schemas and restricted access patterns can simplify distribution, but the guarantees must be checked for the particular system and operation.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 1–8</a></p>"},{"id":"l27-s2","title":"CAP theorem","html":"<p>CAP concerns the incompatibility of linearizable consistency and availability during a network partition. Consistency here means operations behave like one copy respecting real-time order; it is different from ACID consistency about valid database states.</p><p>Availability in the theorem requires operations at nonfailed nodes to complete under its specification. Returning an arbitrary error for every request is not enough to satisfy the intended data-service guarantee. A partition prevents some nodes from communicating reliably.</p><p>During a partition, serving an isolated read can return stale data, while waiting for coordination can sacrifice availability. The common “choose any two” triangle is a simplification; guarantees must be stated for the actual failure and operation model.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 9–10</a></p>"},{"id":"l27-s3","title":"BASE and key-value/column-family models","html":"<p>BASE abbreviates basically available, soft state and eventual consistency. Eventual convergence requires updates to stop or settle and the replication/conflict-resolution mechanism to deliver and reconcile outstanding changes. It does not specify a fixed maximum staleness bound.</p><p>A key-value model addresses an opaque value by key, commonly with get, put and delete. Hashing can determine shard and replica placement. The application must understand the read/write consistency guarantees.</p><p>Column-family models organise sparse, wide records by row keys and column families. They are distinct from the analytical columnar physical layout studied earlier. The source’s system names illustrate categories rather than universal guarantees shared by every product.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 11–14</a></p>"},{"id":"l27-s4","title":"Documents and semi-structured data","html":"<p>A document store exposes the structure of values, allowing predicates on nested fields rather than only lookup by outer key. Documents can contain objects, arrays and scalar values.</p><p>JSON represents objects as key-value collections and arrays as ordered value sequences. Documents can differ in their fields, but applications still rely on structural expectations, validation and indexing choices. Flexible schema does not mean data has no structure.</p><p>The source contrasts relational tables with nested semi-structured documents. These are logical-model differences; actual storage may use binary representations, and relational engines can also expose JSON operations.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 15–17</a></p>"},{"id":"l27-s5","title":"Graphs and workload-driven choice","html":"<p>A property graph represents entities as nodes, relationships as edges and attributes as properties. Queries can describe graph patterns, traversals, connectivity or paths. The source introduces GQL, SQL/PGQ, Cypher and SPARQL as languages associated with graph querying, with distinct data models and syntax.</p><p>Choosing a system requires specifying the required queries, update patterns, transaction boundaries, scale and failure behaviour. A graph model is useful when relationships and traversals are central; documents suit nested aggregates; key-value access suits direct lookup patterns.</p><p>SQL and NoSQL capabilities overlap: relational systems can store documents, and some NoSQL systems provide transactions. Evaluate concrete guarantees rather than inferring them from the category label or popularity chart.</p><p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=18\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PDF pp. 18–21</a></p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Two replicas cannot communicate, and one has acknowledged a new write. What is the problem if a later read at the other replica immediately returns the old value?","options":["It definitely violates durability.","It does not preserve linearizable consistency.","It loses the schema.","It cannot be a NoSQL system."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A read that starts after a successful write but returns the old value does not satisfy linearizability. This is the central trade-off illustrated by CAP."},"source":{"summary":"Lecture 27 covers the motivation for NoSQL, sharding and replication, CAP and BASE, data models, and workload-driven choices. This learning material also explains how to interpret simplified CAP diagrams carefully.","links":[{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=1","label":"PDF pages 1–8"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=9","label":"PDF pages 9–11"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=11","label":"PDF pages 11–14"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=12","label":"PDF pages 12–19"},{"url":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf#page=18","label":"PDF pages 18–21"},{"url":"https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Gilbert/Brewer6.pdf#page=2","label":"Gilbert & Lynch: CAP definitions and theorem, PDF pages 2–4"}]},"coverage":{"pdfUrl":"https://opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/sites/default/files/https/opencourse.inf.ed.ac.uk/adbs/2026/lecture27-nosql.pdf","verified":true,"verifiedPages":"1–21","topics":["NoSQL motivation","sharding","replication","CAP","ACID versus CAP consistency","BASE","key-value","document/JSON","column-family","graph queries","workload choice"],"limitations":["The PDF simplifies the CAP diagram and its description of availability; this lesson provides a more precise interpretation.","The popularity chart is a historical snapshot from the PDF and is not presented as current data."]},"lab":"distributed"}];
window.GLOSSARY=[{"term":"Selection (σ)","definition":"Selection keeps the tuples that satisfy a condition.","lesson":"lecture-03"},{"term":"Projection (π)","definition":"Projection keeps selected attributes. Under set semantics, it also removes duplicate tuples.","lesson":"lecture-03"},{"term":"Buffer pool","definition":"The buffer pool is an area of memory divided into frames that hold database pages.","lesson":"lecture-05"},{"term":"Pin count","definition":"The pin count records active uses of a page. A pinned page cannot be selected for eviction.","lesson":"lecture-05"},{"term":"Slotted page","definition":"A slot directory maps each stable slot ID to the current location of its record within a page.","lesson":"lecture-06"},{"term":"B+ tree","definition":"A B+ tree is a balanced index whose leaves contain data entries and are linked to support range scans.","lesson":"lecture-09"},{"term":"Conflict serializability","definition":"A schedule is conflict-serializable if it is conflict-equivalent to a serial schedule. Its precedence graph must be acyclic.","lesson":"lecture-19"},{"term":"WAL","definition":"Write-ahead logging requires the relevant log records to reach durable storage before their changed data pages.","lesson":"lecture-21"},{"term":"Two-phase commit","definition":"This distributed commit protocol first gathers prepare votes and then announces a decision. It can block after a failure.","lesson":"lecture-22"},{"term":"EXCEPT ALL","definition":"For each tuple, bag difference returns max(mA − mB, 0) copies. SQLite does not support this operator; the Relational Lab simulates it separately.","lesson":"lecture-03"}];
window.SUPPLEMENTS=[{"id":"supplement-28","lecture":null,"order":28,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M11 · Session 1","module":11,"week":11,"suggestedSession":1,"title":"Learned DBMS components: predictions need a systems contract","short":"Cardinality · plan ranking · learned indexes · robust evaluation","group":"11 · Research frontiers","time":45,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"A model can reduce prediction error and still make a database slower. Follow the decision from features to plan to measured execution.","goals":["Locate learning at cardinality estimation, cost/ranking, indexing and configuration boundaries.","Calculate q-error and end-to-end break-even cost.","Design a leakage-resistant evaluation with a safe conventional fallback."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-28-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>A query used to finish in 82 ms. After adding a model it finishes in 69 ms. That sounds useful. But if the next query changes from 5 ms to 11 ms, should the same model still be used? This lesson follows one decision all the way from a predicted row count to the time the user waits.</p>\n<p>You need the idea that different correct query plans can have different costs from lessons 17–18. No model training is needed for the paper exercises. All numerical examples here are original teaching examples.</p>\n<p>This is an original supplemental companion, not an Edinburgh lecture or a verified Canvas assignment. Activities and suggested time are practice guidance. Assigned readings, assessment rules, deadlines and submission arrangements remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s1","title":"Four places to introduce learning","html":"<p>Imagine counting customers before choosing a join. If we expect 500 rows, we may choose a small in-memory strategy; 5,000 actual rows can change its cost. A model that predicts this count is a <strong>cardinality estimator</strong>. Its output helps a decision; it does not replace the actual query result.</p>\n<p>Now suppose all candidate plans are already known. Predicting their costs or ranking them is a different task: a <strong>plan ranker</strong> chooses a plan. A third task, a <strong>learned index</strong>, predicts a key's position. A fourth, a configuration advisor, suggests memory settings, indexes or other resource choices for the workload. These are different inputs and outputs, so a good model for one is not automatically suitable for another.</p>\n<p>Follow an original lookup: prediction → position 420; valid error bound → 6; candidate positions → 414 through 426 inclusive; search these positions → compare the actual keys; return the verified match. Returning the item at 420 without comparison would turn a performance error into a wrong answer. A bound seen only on training keys is not a bound for future keys. Inserts, deletes and changed distributions need an updated correct search procedure or fallback.</p>\n<p>For each component, write its input, permitted output, correctness check, update policy and fallback. Row-count errors normally change performance; unchecked position errors can change answers. Configuration errors can affect concurrent queries as well. This is why the systems contract comes before the choice of neural network.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A learned component is useful only inside a complete algorithm that preserves correctness and accounts for prediction, maintenance and fallback cost.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s2","title":"Worked example: correlation and q-error","html":"<p>Use one Customer snapshot with 100,000 rows. First count City A: 10% means 10,000 rows. Next count commuter-pass holders: 5% means 5,000 rows. Finally ask for both conditions. The key fact is that every pass holder lives in City A, so all 5,000 survive the conjunction.</p>\n<p>What would an independence assumption do? It takes the 10,000 City A rows and keeps 5% of them: 10,000 × 0.05 = 500. The assumption treats pass ownership as equally common inside and outside City A, which contradicts the stated data. We can now name the problem: correlated attributes defeat this independence estimate.</p>\n<p>To compare positive counts c and estimates ĉ, compute both ratios and take the larger: q-error = max(ĉ/c, c/ĉ). At ĉ=500 and c=5,000, the ratios are 0.1 and 10, giving 10. At ĉ=4,000, they are 0.8 and 1.25, giving 1.25. The metric is always at least one. With zeros, division fails, so report a convention such as replacing each value by max(value,1).</p>\n<p>Why still measure execution? The smaller error might occur at a harmless subplan, while another error crosses a memory threshold and causes spilling. Keep the plan, memory use and runtime beside q-error.</p>\n<details><summary>Try a new case: estimate 60,000, actual 15,000. Then reverse the two. What changes?</summary><p>Both have q-error 4: max(4,0.25)=4. The metric is symmetric, but the system cost need not be. Allocating too much memory and spilling after allocating too little are different consequences.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s3","title":"Prediction quality versus end-to-end benefit","html":"<p>Return to the query the user waits for. The conventional path spends 2 ms planning and 80 ms executing: 2+80=82 ms. The learned path spends 9 ms planning and 60 ms executing: 9+60=69 ms. It saves 13 ms end to end. The speedup is 82/69≈1.19; using 80/60≈1.33 silently removes planning from the question.</p>\n<p>Try the same selectors on a shorter query. Conventional execution is 3 ms, giving 2+3=5 ms. Learned execution is 2 ms, giving 9+2=11 ms. Saving 1 ms of execution cannot pay for 7 ms of extra planning. A fallback can therefore be useful even when every chosen plan is legal.</p>\n<p>Training and label collection also consume resources. With a one-time cost of 1,800 seconds and a saving of 0.013 seconds per request, n requests break even when 0.013n≥1,800. Thus n≥138,462 whole requests in this simplified model. Retraining and operating costs raise the requirement; report the amortization assumption rather than hiding it.</p>\n<p>To diagnose selection, measure the fastest plan among the same supplied candidates after running them. This <strong>candidate-plan oracle</strong> uses hindsight and is a diagnostic reference, not a deployable selector or proof of global optimality.</p>\n<details><summary>Candidate oracle 40 ms, learned choice 60 ms, native choice 100 ms: what should be improved?</summary><p>The 20 ms learned-to-oracle execution gap suggests ranking headroom. The 40 ms native-to-learned saving still needs planning overhead added. Even the oracle may miss a better plan that was never proposed. Report workload totals, per-query regressions and timeouts to see whether a few failures outweigh the wins.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s4","title":"Read recent research as testable claims","html":"<p>The arithmetic gives us questions to ask of papers. Lehmann, Sulimov and Stockinger study evaluation of learned optimizers: their comparison depends strongly on how workloads are split, and PostgreSQL wins most tested comparisons. That result concerns those methods and tests; it does not rule out every learned optimizer. GRASP instead predicts cardinalities from query/count observations without raw-table access and studies incomplete or imbalanced join templates. These papers answer different questions.</p>\n<p>A <strong>query template</strong> keeps query structure while replacing changing literal values with placeholders. city='A' and city='B' share city=?; adding AND pass=1 creates another structure. If future work contains unseen joins, holding out only literal values may test the wrong generalization problem.</p>\n<p>There is another useful distinction: an accurate guess is not a safe upper bound. In an original join R(k)⋈S(k), counts for keys k1,k2 are R=(3,1) and S=(2,2). The first key makes 3×2=6 pairs; the second makes 1×2=2; total=8. Cauchy–Schwarz gives √(3²+1²)×√(2²+2²)=√80≈8.94, so the integer output is at most 8. A prediction of 7 is close but is not an upper bound.</p>\n<p>The Lp-norms paper studies more general join-size bounds. Our two-vector calculation explains the distinction, not its full theorem. Read each guarantee with its assumptions; then ask whether you need a point prediction, a one-sided bound, or a correct lookup.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lehmann, Sulimov and Stockinger (PVLDB 2024)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p2519-wu.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GRASP (PVLDB 2025)</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14075\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Join Size Bounds using Lp-Norms on Degree Sequences (PODS 2024)</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s5","title":"Paper-first experiment: generalization without leakage","html":"<p>We now need an experiment that can expose the failure just described. Fix one Customer snapshot and six count queries: Q1 city='A', Q2 city='B', Q3 city='C'; Q4 city='A' AND pass=1, Q5 city='B' AND pass=1, Q6 city='C' AND pass=1. Q1–Q3 share a template; Q4–Q6 share a second.</p>\n<p>For a new-literal test on known templates, train on Q1/Q4, tune choices on Q2/Q5, and reserve Q3/Q6 untouched for the final score. For a new-template test, instead train on Q1, tune on Q2/Q3, and hold all Q4–Q6 out. These are alternative designs. Letting Q4 influence tuning in the second design destroys the unseen-template test. A time-held-out workload needs its date boundary fixed before inspecting results.</p>\n<p>Optional implementation can use one table, a CPU regression tool and a small label budget. Record split IDs, observed counts, estimates, q-errors, plans, timings, dataset hash and seed. Compare native estimates, a histogram or simple regression, and the proposed model with equal label budgets. Include collection/training, inference, execution, timeouts and worst regressions.</p>\n<p>To learn why the method works, change one thing at a time. Halve labels while holding snapshot, split, model and timing fixed: that is an <strong>ablation</strong>. Test workload overlap separately. Unsupported SQL, unfamiliar inputs or inference errors require a conventional fallback; confidence is not a correctness proof. GPU training is optional, and a controlled negative result is still useful.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Evaluate the workload you want to generalize to, not just a convenient random split.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-28-s6","title":"Seminar exercise and answer","html":"<p>A report says median q-error is 1.4 and mean execution speedup is 2×. It omits inference, training and three timed-out queries. Before accepting the headline, separate what was measured from what the claim needs.</p>\n<details><summary>Write a fair conclusion and a next experiment.</summary><p>The reported estimation score and execution-only comparison may improve on the selected workload. End-to-end benefit and robustness are not established. First request a timeout-aware workload total and planning/collection/training costs. Then freeze a template-held-out or time-held-out test with comparable baseline tuning. A timeout cannot be removed simply because it lowers the mean. This experiment would distinguish a useful selector from a favorable reporting boundary.</p></details>\n<p>Finish by stating the contract in one sentence: this component makes this decision, on this supported workload, with these checks and fallback. That sentence should be meaningful even to a reader who does not know the model architecture.</p>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A learned selector saves 4 ms of execution but adds 7 ms of planning. What follows?","options":["It improves end-to-end latency by 3 ms.","Q-error must be below one.","The query result must be incorrect.","It worsens end-to-end latency by 3 ms, before amortized training cost."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Added planning is 7 ms and saved execution is 4 ms, so the total grows by 7−4=3 ms before training is amortized. A legal plan can be slower; this performance change says nothing by itself about SQL result correctness.","id":"supplement-28-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["lqo","grasp","lp"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-28-s1","supplement-28-s2","supplement-28-s3","supplement-28-s4","supplement-28-s5","supplement-28-s6"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf","label":"Is Your Learned Query Optimizer Behaving As You Expect? A Machine Learning Perspective · Published peer-reviewed research / evaluation"},{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p2519-wu.pdf","label":"Data-Agnostic Cardinality Learning from Imperfect Workloads · Published peer-reviewed research"},{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14075","label":"Join Size Bounds using Lp-Norms on Degree Sequences · Accepted PODS 2024 paper; linked arXiv is an author manuscript first posted in 2023"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":11,"week":11,"syllabusTopics":["learned DBMS components","cardinality estimation","learned cost models and indexes","robust experimental design","recent SIGMOD/VLDB/ICDE/PODS reading"],"topics":["learned DBMS components","cardinality estimation","learned cost models and indexes","robust experimental design","recent SIGMOD/VLDB/ICDE/PODS reading"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["lqo","grasp","lp"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-29","lecture":null,"order":29,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M11 · Session 2","module":11,"week":11,"suggestedSession":2,"title":"Vector databases: the recall, memory and latency contract","short":"Exact search · HNSW/IVF/PQ · GPU graphs · filtered top-k","group":"11 · Research frontiers","time":45,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"Approximation is a choice about which candidates you can afford to miss. A vector database must also handle filters, versions, updates and access rules.","goals":["Define exact top-k and ANN recall using the same metric and filter.","Compare graph traversal, inverted-file pruning and vector compression.","Design a recall-matched single-GPU experiment and explain multi-GPU limits."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-29-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>Suppose a search returns five documents in 2 ms. Another returns five in 3 ms. Before choosing the faster search, we need to know which documents should have been returned. This lesson builds that answer first, then shows how an index trades search work for missed neighbors.</p>\n<p>You only need vectors as lists of numbers and the purpose of an index. Numerical examples are original teaching examples. GPU implementation is optional. This supplemental companion is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; assigned readings, assessment rules, deadlines and submission arrangements remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s1","title":"Start with exact semantics","html":"<p>Start with q=(1,0) and two objects A=(1,0), B=(0,1). Squared Euclidean distance adds squared coordinate differences: d²(q,A)=0²+0²=0; d²(q,B)=1²+(−1)²=2. Smaller means closer. Inner product adds coordinate products: q·A=1, q·B=0; here larger means more similar.</p>\n<p>Both objects and q have unit length. For unit vectors, d²=2−2(q·x), so maximizing inner product also minimizes distance and cosine distance. Without normalization the rankings can differ. Declare metric, preprocessing, numeric representation and tie-breaking before making a reference answer.</p>\n<p>An exact top-k search chooses the k best eligible IDs under that definition. An approximate nearest-neighbor search (ANN) saves work by not examining everything or by approximating distances. If the exact top-5 is {A,B,C,D,E} and ANN returns {A,C,E,G,H}, their intersection is {A,C,E}; recall@5=3/5=0.6. Returning five objects does not mean finding all five correct neighbors.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Separate embedding relevance, nearest-neighbor recall and downstream task accuracy. They measure different failure modes.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s2","title":"Three complementary design choices","html":"<p>Why can ANN avoid a full scan? One strategy stores neighbor links. Starting at a graph node, compare nearby candidates, follow promising links and keep a shortlist. Exploring more nodes spends more distance calculations and memory traffic, usually improving recall. Construction and updates also cost work.</p>\n<p>A second strategy groups vectors into clusters. Search only selected clusters rather than the entire collection: this is an inverted-file index (IVF). More probes inspect more candidates; a query near a cluster boundary can lose a close neighbor in an unvisited cluster.</p>\n<p>A third strategy replaces full vectors with compact codes: quantization. It saves memory and estimates distance cheaply, but estimates can reverse two near-tied objects. Reranking a shortlist with original vectors corrects their distances, not neighbors omitted from that shortlist. These mechanisms can combine: IVF with quantized codes, or a graph with compressed vectors.</p>\n<p>Compare against an exact scan, especially with small collections or large GPU batches. A vector database must also persist IDs, metadata, deletes and embedding versions, enforce access rules and coordinate concurrent operations. A faster search kernel alone does not establish a faster complete database.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pgvector official repository documentation</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s3","title":"Worked example: recall and filtering","html":"<p>Now change eligibility. Keep the unfiltered exact top-5 {A,B,C,D,E}, but suppose a tenant filter permits only {B,D,F,G,H}. We must compute the best neighbors inside the permitted subset. The unfiltered reference is now the wrong answer to a different question.</p>\n<p>Filtering after ANN can leave too few results. In an original model with independent 1% acceptance, 100 candidates leave 100×0.01=1 survivor on average. Retrieving 1,000 gives an expectation of ten, not a guarantee. Geometry and metadata can correlate, invalidating independence. Possible responses include iterative expansion, filter-aware search or exact search of the eligible subset.</p>\n<p>Enforce access control before exposing protected results or payloads. Define how snapshots, deleted IDs and changed embeddings become visible; then test freshness separately from recall.</p>\n<details><summary>After filtering, three returned IDs all belong to exact filtered top-10. Is recall 1?</summary><p>No. With at least ten eligible items, intersection size is three and denominator is ten: recall@10=0.3. Precision among those three is perfect, but seven required neighbors were missed. If fewer than k eligible objects exist, state a separate denominator convention.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s4","title":"Read two recent approaches","html":"<p>The preceding mechanisms help separate two research contributions. CAGRA designs GPU graph construction and traversal. Its results depend on datasets, baselines, hardware, search settings and batch sizes. They are author-reported comparisons, not promised H100 performance in this exercise.</p>\n<p>RaBitQ focuses on compact representation and bounded distance estimation. Its main coordinate code uses one bit per dimension, but surrounding scale information, IDs, links and optional reranking vectors still take space. An error bound on a distance estimate is not a promise that arbitrary candidate search returns the exact top-k.</p>\n<p>Try this reasoning: if two candidates' true distances are 1.00 and 1.01, estimation error can swap their order. Even perfect reranking cannot recover a true neighbor never considered. Ask whether your bottleneck is traversal, representation capacity, bandwidth, arithmetic or construction before selecting a paper's mechanism. Read implementation guides at a pinned version; current software need not reproduce the original artifact.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.15136\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAGRA (ICDE 2024)</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12497\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RaBitQ (SIGMOD 2024)</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s5","title":"H100-sized mini-experiment: one GPU first","html":"<p>First choose the quality requirement. In an original paper checkpoint, low effort returns {A,C,E,G,H} in a hypothetical 2 ms; high effort returns {A,B,C,D,E} in 3 ms. Against exact {A,B,C,D,E}, recall is 0.6 versus 1.0. At required recall≥0.95 only high effort qualifies. The 2 ms point cannot be advertised as faster at equal quality.</p>\n<p>Before a GPU run, budget one million 768-dimensional FP32 vectors: 1,000,000×768×4=3,072,000,000 bytes=3.072 GB decimal. Degree-64 links with four-byte IDs add 1,000,000×64×4=0.256 GB. The sum 3.328 GB omits workspace, copies, buffers, allocator and other metadata; it is not measured peak memory.</p>\n<p>Optional one-H100 work can compare exact GPU scan, IVF-Flat or IVF-PQ, and CAGRA at matched metric, data and recall. Separate validation tuning from held-out queries. Measure build time, peak memory, batch size, latency distribution, throughput and host-device transfers. Check actual free memory and pinned software/platform requirements first. Two H100s require explicit placement and communication measurements; they do not automatically pool memory or double speed.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/api-guides/indexing-guide/cagra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NVIDIA cuVS: CAGRA guide</a>; <a href=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies</a>; <a href=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/installation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NVIDIA cuVS: Installation</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p><p>The current cuVS installation guidance has Linux packages and a WSL2 route for Windows, rather than native Windows support. Pin a release and compatible GPU/CUDA stack before running. A two-device extension should distinguish replicas from partitioned indexes: replicas may raise throughput, whereas partitions need routing and result merging. Include update/delete freshness, construction cost and peak memory in either case.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-29-s6","title":"Exercises, failure cases and takeaways","html":"<p>Keep one record per configuration: dataset and query split, preprocessing, index version, build/search settings, recall, timing boundary, batch size and memory. This lets another reader see why a setting is useful rather than remember one QPS number.</p>\n<details><summary>A gives 20,000 queries/s at recall 0.80; B gives 12,000 at 0.96. Which wins at equal quality?</summary><p>The measurements cannot answer that. Sweep settings and compare where both meet the same target, or show a Pareto frontier: points for which improving one metric requires worsening another. A is faster at its reported, lower-quality point.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does a one-bit code make the entire database 32× smaller than FP32?</summary><p>Only the raw coordinate-code ratio is 32:1. Add scales, centroids, links, IDs, metadata and any originals used for reranking. Measure the complete footprint and construction peak. Also check filtered queries, updates and near ties before transferring the conclusion to a new workload.</p></details><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>An honest ANN result reports recall, workload, batch size, latency/throughput, build cost and memory together.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"The exact top-10 and returned top-10 share eight IDs. What is recall@10?","options":["1.0","It cannot be computed without distance values.","0.2","0.8"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The intersection contains eight required IDs, and ten exact eligible neighbors were requested. Recall@10=8/10=0.8. The two extra returned IDs do not recover the two missing required neighbors.","id":"supplement-29-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["cagra","rabitq","cuvs","cuvsbench","cuvsinstall","pgvector"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-29-s1","supplement-29-s2","supplement-29-s3","supplement-29-s4","supplement-29-s5","supplement-29-s6"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.15136","label":"CAGRA: Highly Parallel Graph Construction and Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search for GPUs · Accepted ICDE 2024 paper; linked author manuscript v2 revised July 2024, first posted August 2023"},{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12497","label":"RaBitQ: Quantizing High-Dimensional Vectors with a Theoretical Error Bound for Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search · Accepted SIGMOD 2024 paper; linked author manuscript explicitly confirms acceptance"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/api-guides/indexing-guide/cagra","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: CAGRA guide · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/installation","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Installation · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11; version-sensitive"},{"url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector","label":"pgvector official repository documentation · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11; version-sensitive"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":11,"week":11,"syllabusTopics":["vector databases and ANN search","GPU graph search","quantization","filtered search","H100 experiment design"],"topics":["vector databases and ANN search","GPU graph search","quantization","filtered search","H100 experiment design"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["cagra","rabitq","cuvs","cuvsbench","cuvsinstall","pgvector"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-30","lecture":null,"order":30,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M11 · Session 3","module":11,"week":12,"suggestedSession":3,"title":"LLMs for data systems: semantic operators with measurable quality","short":"Text-to-SQL · semantic analytics · cascades · evaluation","group":"11 · Research frontiers","time":45,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"Treat each model call as a costly, fallible operator with an explicit input, output and evaluation target.","goals":["Distinguish LLM-assisted database control from LLM-powered data processing.","Calculate filter/cascade costs without assuming quality is unchanged.","Evaluate semantic pipelines against human labels and reference algorithms separately."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-30-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>You have 20,000 support notes and want the 2025 notes about battery overheating. Reading every note with a large model sounds straightforward, but how would you know it found the right notes, and which work can safely be skipped? We will answer those questions before discussing a system name.</p>\n<p>Recall filtering and operator order from lessons 2–3 and 15. Paper calculations require no model or paid service. All traces and prices below are hypothetical teaching examples. This is supplemental practice, not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; assigned readings, assessment rules, deadlines and submission arrangements remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s1","title":"Two directions, two correctness problems","html":"<p>Start with a familiar SQL request: find overheating notes from 2025. An LLM might draft SQL for us. The DBMS can check syntax and execute it correctly, but an incorrect join may duplicate notes or a wrong date boundary may exclude December. Valid execution does not prove that the question was interpreted correctly. This is an LLM assisting DBMS control.</p>\n<p>Now keep SQL's date filter but ask a model whether each note describes overheating. The model is acting on data as an operator. We must define positive, negative and uncertain labels, require evidence spans, and decide how errors are checked. Extraction, semantic joins, ranking and aggregation have similar issues: a natural-language instruction is not yet an unambiguous correctness rule.</p>\n<p>Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) first retrieves context and then generates an answer. Retrieval can miss evidence and generation can contradict it; this does not supply transactional database guarantees. Record snapshot, model/embedding versions, prompts and retrieval settings. Document text remains input data, including any text pretending to give tool instructions. Keep question interpretation, SQL execution, semantic labels and grounded answers as separate checks.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>SQL execution correctness, interpretation of the question, semantic-label quality and grounded answer quality are separate checks.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s2","title":"Worked example: push a cheap filter before an expensive operator","html":"<p>Work through the 20,000-note collection. A trustworthy date field retains 4,000 notes from 2025. Classifying every note at an assumed $0.002 costs 20,000×0.002=$40. Date filtering first costs 4,000×0.002=$8. We saved calls because excluded dates cannot answer this specific question. If the date field is wrong or excluded notes supply necessary context, that rewrite needs reconsideration.</p>\n<p>Next let a cheap model classify all 4,000 at $0.0002 each: $0.80. Route 25%, or 1,000 uncertain notes, to the expensive model: $2.00. Total=$2.80 before calibration, labeling, retries and optimization. A confident false negative is not routed, so the saving can hide missed overheating notes.</p>\n<p>Count against independent labels. True positives (TP) are real overheating notes found; false positives (FP) are other notes incorrectly included; false negatives (FN) are real ones missed. Precision=TP/(TP+FP) asks how trustworthy the returned set is. Recall=TP/(TP+FN) asks how much of the required set was found.</p>\n<details><summary>TP=400, FP=50, FN=100. Does the cheap cascade meet recall≥0.9?</summary><p>Precision=400/450≈0.889; recall=400/500=0.8, so it fails. Lower cost does not fix missing required positives. Choose routing thresholds on a calibration set, then test on separate labeled data. Use public/synthetic data or approved local inference; do not assume permission to transmit private documents to an external provider.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s3","title":"Recent systems and what their guarantees mean","html":"<p>The example shows why cost optimization needs a quality definition. LOTUS offers semantic operators parameterized by natural language, with optimized implementations and statistical accuracy guarantees relative to a specified high-quality reference algorithm. Reference agreement and factual truth are different: two models can agree on the same wrong label. Guarantees for individual operators also need care when composed into a pipeline.</p>\n<p>DocETL studies document-processing rewrites. Splitting a long document or decomposing a task can expose evidence a single large prompt misses. Its reported evaluation covers four tasks; a rewrite that helps them need not help every document collection.</p>\n<p>Predict a failure before implementing: summarizing each chunk may remove a rare exception; deduplication may merge two distinct events; limiting join candidates may remove the only true match. Model choice, prompts, chunking, batching, selective calls and reuse therefore alter both cost and potentially meaning. Read the reference algorithm, label policy, evaluation splits and optimization cost beside any speedup.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p4171-patel.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LOTUS (PVLDB 2025)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p3035-shankar.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DocETL (PVLDB 2025)</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s4","title":"A complete quality and cost evaluation","html":"<p>Make the labels concrete before collecting a score. For example: include “battery became too hot to touch”; exclude “battery did not overheat”; mark an unreadable or conflicting note uncertain and retain its evidence span. Have independent annotators resolve disagreements on a sample rather than silently deciding from model output.</p>\n<p>Compare deterministic rules where meaningful, one fixed-model pipeline and the optimized pipeline on the same task. Split by source document or entity so near-duplicates cannot leak between development, calibration and test. Choose prompts and thresholds before reading final test labels.</p>\n<p>Keep raw output and model/prompt revisions. Report precision, recall, F1 or field errors, important subgroups and unsupported claims. Separately count calls, input/output tokens, assumed price, optimizer search, retries, wall time, p95 and peak memory. A fixed seed need not make every backend identical; repeat when variability affects the decision.</p>\n<details><summary>The optimized pipeline agrees with its reference LLM on 98% of notes. Is factual accuracy 98%?</summary><p>No. That is reference agreement. A separate factual or human-labeled check is needed because the reference can share the optimized model's mistakes. Preserve the 98% agreement result under its correct name.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s5","title":"H100-sized mini-experiment: semantic-filter batching and cascades","html":"<p>Trace four held-out notes with independent labels positive, negative, positive, negative. Cheap outputs are positive, negative, uncertain, negative. Only note three goes to the expensive model. If it says positive, final labels recover both positives with one expensive call. If cheap output three had confidently said negative, the expensive stage would never see the missed positive. This is why confidence routing needs recall calibration.</p>\n<p>Optional local work can compare batch sizes and context limits with a small licensed open-weight model on public/synthetic documents. Eight billion parameters at two bytes each already require about 16 GB just for weights. The attention key/value cache, activations, workspace and allocator add memory, depending on context length, batch, architecture and implementation. Inspect actual H100 capacity and pinned serving-engine requirements.</p>\n<p>Hold prompts, model and quality evaluation fixed while comparing single-model inference with the cascade; load sequentially if needed. Measure complete cost and quality. Two-device replicas and tensor/model parallelism solve different problems and add orchestration or communication; two H100s are not automatically one memory pool. This exercise does not train a foundation model or promise a novel result.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/configuration/optimization/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">vLLM Optimization and Tuning</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-30-s6","title":"From a prototype to a safe research claim","html":"<p>A useful conclusion names the corpus, label policy, model version, quality target, measured cost and exceptions. “Cheaper” without those conditions leaves the reader unable to decide whether the same task was solved.</p>\n<details><summary>You chose a prompt after inspecting every test error, then scored that same set again. How can the study be repaired?</summary><p>That set has become development data. Freeze the prompt and test on a new held-out set, or report the result as exploratory rather than an unbiased final score. Keep the search budget and alternatives tried. The extra search cost belongs in the experiment even if only the final pipeline is deployed.</p></details>\n<p>Before recommending a shortcut, explain which work it omits and which answers it might change. That causal statement connects query optimization to semantic processing without assuming exact relational equivalence.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Optimize quality, cost and latency jointly. Every shortcut needs an explicit statement of what it may change.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"LOTUS-style agreement with a reference algorithm most directly measures what?","options":["Statistical fidelity to the chosen reference behavior.","Guaranteed factual truth.","Serializable database isolation.","Exact nearest-neighbor recall."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The reference algorithm defines the target for agreement. If both reference and optimized model make the same factual mistake, they still agree. Human/factual truth and effects of composing several operators therefore require separate checks.","id":"supplement-30-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["lotus","docetl","vllm"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-30-s1","supplement-30-s2","supplement-30-s3","supplement-30-s4","supplement-30-s5","supplement-30-s6"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p4171-patel.pdf","label":"Semantic Operators and Their Optimization: Enabling LLM-Based Data Processing with Accuracy Guarantees in LOTUS · Published peer-reviewed research; use published title, not earlier preprint title"},{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p3035-shankar.pdf","label":"DocETL: Agentic Query Rewriting and Evaluation for Complex Document Processing · Published peer-reviewed research"},{"url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/stable/configuration/optimization/","label":"vLLM Optimization and Tuning · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11; version-sensitive"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":11,"week":12,"syllabusTopics":["LLMs for data systems","semantic operators","text-to-SQL validation","quality-cost trade-offs","H100 inference experiment design"],"topics":["LLMs for data systems","semantic operators","text-to-SQL validation","quality-cost trade-offs","H100 inference experiment design"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["lotus","docetl","vllm"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-31","lecture":null,"order":31,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M11 · Session 4","module":11,"week":12,"suggestedSession":4,"title":"Cloud-native and disaggregated DBMSs: locality becomes a protocol","short":"Compute/storage separation · ownership · logging · failure recovery","group":"11 · Research frontiers","time":45,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"Separating resources makes scaling more flexible. It also moves familiar memory and recovery decisions across a network.","goals":["Compare shared-nothing, shared-storage and compute-memory-storage designs.","Trace page ownership, write-ahead logging and recovery responsibilities.","Estimate remote-access costs and design a workload/failure experiment."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-31-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>A client received “commit successful,” then the compute node failed before its modified page reached storage. Another node reads an older page. Where can the acknowledged change be recovered? We will follow that one page before drawing a cloud architecture.</p>\n<p>Recall buffer management, concurrency and recovery from lessons 5 and 19–22. The numerical and protocol traces are original simplified examples, not the exact implementation of any named system. This supplemental practice is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; assigned readings, assessment rules, deadlines and submission arrangements remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s1","title":"Draw the resource and responsibility boundaries","html":"<p>First put compute, cached pages and durable state on paper. In a shared-nothing design, each worker owns a partition and its local resources; cross-partition operations communicate. Separating compute from storage lets compute nodes access a durable storage service. A further memory service can hold shared buffers or coordination state independently of compute.</p>\n<p>Why separate them? We may want more compute without copying the entire durable dataset, or more storage without buying the same compute ratio. But a new compute node has a cold cache. Reads may now cross a network; membership, routing and ownership still need coordination. “Serverless” hides some operations from the user, not the servers or their failure modes.</p>\n<p>For every box, label owned state, replication, consistency and failure behavior. A cache is not automatically authoritative, and authoritative state is not automatically durable. CXL memory expansion and network RDMA are different mechanisms; drawing a remote-memory box does not specify either protocol. These distinctions let us ask precisely where an update survives.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Independent scaling does not imply independent correctness. Coordination and durability contracts still connect the layers.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s2","title":"Worked example: latency, bandwidth and caching","html":"<p>A query needs 1,000 remote pages of 8 KiB each: 1,000×8,192=8,192,000 bytes. At an assumed usable 2 GiB/s, transfer time alone is 8,192,000/(2×1,073,741,824)≈3.81 ms. Now add a 0.2 ms round trip per request. If each request waits for the previous one, 1,000×0.2=200 ms of latency dominates.</p>\n<p>Allow 100 outstanding requests. In an idealized model there are ten rounds, so the latency component is 10×0.2=2 ms. The bytes still require 3.81 ms. Overlap means we cannot blindly add the two; time is at least their maximum before CPU work, contention, queues and other overhead.</p>\n<p>With a warm 90% hit rate, only 100 pages go remote in this toy model. Comparing a warm old node with a cold new node would confound cache state and architecture.</p>\n<details><summary>Remote bytes halve but a chain of 100 dependent requests remains. Must time halve?</summary><p>No. The serial request latency can remain dominant. Measure bytes, dependency depth, concurrent requests, queues and CPU separately; report cold transition and warm steady state.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s3","title":"Multi-primary writes and recovery","html":"<p>Follow page P. Node A currently owns write authority at generation 7. Its cache changes P from 10 to 11 and creates log evidence. The durable page may still say 10. Before replying “commit,” the protocol must make its required log and commit evidence durable: write-ahead logging (WAL) separates durable evidence from delayed page writing.</p>\n<p>A fails before flushing P. Recovery checks durable commit status and uses the log to reconstruct 11. It grants B current authority at generation 8. If A returns still believing generation 7 is valid, its old writes must be rejected. This rejection of obsolete authority is <strong>fencing</strong>. A network-partitioned owner can need fencing even while still alive.</p>\n<p>Shared storage alone does not supply this protocol. A cached copy is not permission to write. Replicated old pages cannot recover a newer acknowledged value without the necessary evidence. With independent logs, locally increasing numbers alone may not give a sufficient cross-node replay order; interacting changes need declared ordering/dependencies.</p>\n<details><summary>A fails before commit evidence is durable. Must 11 survive as committed?</summary><p>No. Recovery determines commit status using the protocol; an uncommitted change may be undone or ignored. Durable commit, page version and write authority answer separate questions. Acknowledging an update held only in volatile private memory would risk losing a promised change.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s4","title":"Read two concrete multi-primary designs","html":"<p>Use the trace to read real designs. PolarDB-MP separates memory and storage; its PMFS service handles transaction visibility, shared buffers and locks, and the paper describes log ordering and recovery. It is a SIGMOD 2024 industrial paper in the Companion, not a PODS paper or SIGMOD research-track paper.</p>\n<p>GaussDB also separates compute, memory and storage. Its memory layer manages global buffers/locks; page ownership, placement and routing help reduce transfers. Compare where authority lives and what moves during contention rather than assuming the architectures are interchangeable.</p>\n<p>Neon's linked 2022 architecture account separates compute, WAL safekeepers and page serving. Use it to identify durability responsibilities, not to infer current pricing, SLA or every present implementation detail. For each paper, redraw the path of one read, one committed write and one failure. That makes unfamiliar system names answer a question we already understand.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://cighao.com/papers/polardb_mp.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PolarDB-MP (SIGMOD 2024 Industry)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p3786-li.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GaussDB (PVLDB 2024)</a>; <a href=\"https://neon.com/blog/architecture-decisions-in-neon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Neon’s official architecture account</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s5","title":"Mini-experiment: isolate the benefit and the network cost","html":"<p>An accessible experiment can isolate remote access rather than rebuild a production database. Compare local access, remote access without caching, and remote access with a fixed cache budget. Use sequential and random requests, then vary working-set size and skew. Sweep assumed latency separately from bandwidth; record latency distribution, throughput, bytes, cache hits, CPU and concurrency.</p>\n<p>For a multi-writer prototype, increase hot-page contention and count ownership transfers, aborts/retries and coordination time. In a disposable sandbox, crash compute after durable commit but before page flush; verify committed values and measure recovery. A toy test does not certify high availability.</p>\n<p>Separate fixed-total-resource scale-out from experiments adding memory/CPU. A single-machine delay model tests a mechanism, not RDMA, CXL or production control planes. H100s do not replace the CPU, storage and network resources needed here. State the narrow claim your available environment can actually test.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-31-s6","title":"Exercises and synthesis","html":"<p>Return to the opening failure. To preserve an acknowledged update, identify durable evidence, commit status, valid page version and current write authority. Independent resource scaling rearranges their costs without removing these requirements.</p>\n<details><summary>A new node doubles cache capacity and throughput rises 1.6×. What does this establish?</summary><p>It establishes that measured combined change, not architectural efficiency alone. Compare equal total memory, disclose CPU/network differences, use a working set exceeding the original cache, and measure cold transition as well as steady state.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Storage is replicated. Is compute recovery unnecessary?</summary><p>No. Copies may contain old pages. Recovery still needs the right durable evidence, commit decision, ownership/version rules, replay and fencing. Test the failure path separately from peak throughput.</p></details><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A useful cloud-native evaluation includes transitions and failures, not only steady-state peak throughput.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What does compute-storage disaggregation alone guarantee?","options":["Linear scaling with compute nodes.","A separation of resource roles; other guarantees need explicit protocols.","No need for concurrency control.","Zero cold-start overhead."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disaggregation is an architectural separation. Scalability, correctness, recovery and latency depend on mechanisms and workload.","id":"supplement-31-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["polar","gauss","neon"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-31-s1","supplement-31-s2","supplement-31-s3","supplement-31-s4","supplement-31-s5","supplement-31-s6"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://cighao.com/papers/polardb_mp.pdf","label":"PolarDB-MP: A Multi-Primary Cloud-Native Database via Disaggregated Shared Memory · Published industrial paper; not a SIGMOD research-track or PODS paper"},{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p3786-li.pdf","label":"GaussDB: A Cloud-Native Multi-Primary Database with Compute-Memory-Storage Disaggregation · Published industrial/system paper"},{"url":"https://neon.com/blog/architecture-decisions-in-neon","label":"Architecture decisions in Neon · Official engineering design account; historical architecture example"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":11,"week":12,"syllabusTopics":["cloud-native DBMSs","disaggregated compute/memory/storage","multi-primary ownership","WAL and recovery","experimental scope"],"topics":["cloud-native DBMSs","disaggregated compute/memory/storage","multi-primary ownership","WAL and recovery","experimental scope"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["polar","gauss","neon"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-32","lecture":null,"order":32,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M12 · Session 1","module":12,"week":13,"suggestedSession":1,"title":"Project clinic: turn a topic into a falsifiable research question","short":"Scope · hypothesis · literature matrix · consultation","group":"12 · Project clinic","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"“Use a GPU for databases” is a topic. A research question names a mechanism, a comparison, a workload and a possible failure.","goals":["Write a bounded research claim with a falsification condition.","Connect a proposed mechanism to an observed bottleneck.","Prepare concrete evidence and decisions for an instructor consultation."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-32-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>“I want to study vector databases” names a topic, but does not tell us what result could change our mind. We will turn it into a question using one mechanism: waiting briefly to search several incoming queries together.</p>\n<p>Bring one mechanism from lessons 28–31. The charter below is an original planning example, not a measured research result. This supplemental clinic is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment. Instructor appointments, required deliverables, assessment rules and deadlines remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-32-s1","title":"The research-question contract","html":"<p>Imagine queries arrive one at a time and the GPU spends much of its time waiting. A small waiting window might collect several queries and use the device better. But the first query must now wait for the others. We have a possible benefit and an immediate cost, so we can ask: under what arrival rates does batching improve throughput while staying within a p95 response-latency budget?</p>\n<p>Here p95 is the response time at the declared 95th percentile; state how it is computed. Fix the dataset, hardware, metric and recall target. Compare immediate dispatch and a tuned batching baseline. Write what would count against the proposal: no benefit at matched p95, or recall below target.</p>\n<p>This makes the claim <strong>falsifiable</strong>: an allowed observation can show it false. An implementation milestone such as “build an ANN server” cannot do that. The output of the project can be a bounded operating range, including losing cases, rather than a universal win.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A narrow question with a credible baseline is stronger than a broad system with no testable claim.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-32-s2","title":"Use a causal chain to justify the mechanism","html":"<p>Before building, trace the suspected cause: tiny batches → little parallel work per launch → low device utilization → a waiting window may increase batch size → queue delay may increase response time. Each arrow needs evidence, not just a plausible story.</p>\n<p>Record batch sizes, utilization, queue delay, device execution and arrival-to-response latency. If preprocessing on the host is already the bottleneck, faster device search may barely change the user's wait. A minimal prototype should test the most uncertain arrow first; a short feasibility time box is a planning choice, not a course deadline.</p>\n<details><summary>Turn “LLMs improve query optimization” into a testable question.</summary><p>For example: on held-out multi-table templates, can an LLM choose among the same ten legal plans faster end to end than the native optimizer, including selection time and without extra timeouts? Add a simple non-LLM ranker. This fixes decision, domain, candidates, baseline, cost boundary and failure condition.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-32-s3","title":"Build a literature-and-evidence matrix","html":"<p>A related paper is useful when it can change a project decision. For each close paper, record exact title, venue/year and publication status, then problem, assumption, mechanism, strongest baseline, workload, metric, artifact and limitation. Finish with “what differs from my question?” and “what would reproducing this require?”</p>\n<p>For our batching question, a paper may report high throughput only for large preformed batches. That supports the device mechanism but does not measure how long online users wait to form a batch. A learned-optimizer paper's random query split may likewise not answer unseen-template generalization.</p>\n<p>Keep author-reported measurements separate from your own. An arXiv manuscript can be an accepted paper when a primary record confirms acceptance; otherwise label its actual status. This bibliography is supplemental and does not invent an instructor-assigned reading list. Reading the protocol as carefully as the abstract prevents borrowing a claim under incompatible conditions.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Is Your Learned Query Optimizer Behaving As You Expect? A Machine Learning Perspective</a>; <a href=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-32-s4","title":"Worked example: a one-page research charter","html":"<p>Write the charter in the order a reader needs. Question: when does microbatching improve end-to-end ANN throughput under a p95 budget? Motivation: preliminary traces suggest low batch-1 utilization, which must be measured. Mechanism: cap how long a query waits while arrivals accumulate. Baselines: immediate dispatch and tuned fixed-size batching with identical search parameters.</p>\n<p>Vary arrival rate, waiting cap and difficulty; hold dataset, GPU and quality target fixed. Collect queue and execution time, arrival-to-response p50/p95/p99, QPS, recall and failures. Falsification: gains disappear at matched p95 or difficult queries miss the recall target. Deliver a reproducible plot showing both useful and losing regimes.</p>\n<p>Finally inspect the load generator. A closed-loop client sends the next request only after a reply; slowing the server reduces offered load and can hide queue growth. An open-loop client schedules arrivals independently. Choose the model matching your claim, verify the generator keeps up, and record dropped or failed arrivals. Otherwise a queueing result may really describe the client.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-32-s5","title":"Prepare an instructor consultation that can change the project","html":"<p>A consultation should resolve a decision, so bring the one-sentence question, bottleneck trace, closest paper and precise difference. Add one preliminary result or failure with configuration and uncertainty. Ask two concrete questions, such as whether the baseline is fair and whether scope should prioritize filtered recall or freshness.</p>\n<p>After the discussion, record the actual advice, evidence, decision, owner and next check. An informal suggestion does not become a grading rule. Follow actual appointment and document instructions when provided.</p>\n<details><summary>The proposal needs four GPUs but only one is available. What is a rigorous rescope?</summary><p>Study a single-device mechanism or an explicitly controlled model of communication overhead and narrow the claim accordingly. Do not call it reproduction of four-device scaling. Record the resource limit and ask whether the narrowed question fits the instructor's real expectations. A smaller answer supported by evidence is more useful than an untestable large promise.</p></details><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Bring a decision and the evidence needed to make it, rather than a list of activities.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest project starting point?","options":["Build the fastest possible database.","Use every available GPU.","Implement a paper without reading its evaluation.","Test a specific mechanism against a fair baseline under a stated workload and constraint."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A testable claim connects scope, mechanism and evidence; implementation size alone does not make a research contribution.","id":"supplement-32-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["lqo","cuvsbench"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-32-s1","supplement-32-s2","supplement-32-s3","supplement-32-s4","supplement-32-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf","label":"Is Your Learned Query Optimizer Behaving As You Expect? A Machine Learning Perspective · Published peer-reviewed research / evaluation"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":12,"week":13,"syllabusTopics":["project clinic","supervised research","research question","literature review","instructor consultation"],"topics":["project clinic","supervised research","research question","literature review","instructor consultation"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["lqo","cuvsbench"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-33","lecture":null,"order":33,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M12 · Session 2","module":12,"week":13,"suggestedSession":2,"title":"Project clinic: trustworthy baselines and measurements","short":"Workload design · tuning fairness · latency · uncertainty","group":"12 · Project clinic","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"A result is only as convincing as the comparison that produced it. Design the evaluation before you optimize the system.","goals":["Choose baselines that isolate the claimed improvement.","Distinguish workload-total, per-query and tail metrics.","Design repeatable timing, split and timeout policies."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-33-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>Some queries improve and one gets slower. Has the system improved? We will calculate the total before choosing a summary, then design a measurement that cannot hide the slow query.</p>\n<p>Bring the bounded question from lesson 32. All timings below are original hypothetical examples. This clinic is supplemental practice, not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; instructor deliverables, assessment rules, deadlines and submission arrangements remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-33-s1","title":"Choose what the baseline is supposed to answer","html":"<p>A baseline must answer a question. A native or widely used system tests practical benefit. A simple heuristic asks whether complexity is necessary. Removing the new component while leaving a coherent system tests its contribution: an ablation. A fastest-measured candidate oracle shows headroom, but its hindsight is not deployable performance.</p>\n<p>For batching, comparing against an untuned server with a different recall target leaves several explanations for a win. Match resources, quality, dataset, splits and tuning effort; label defaults as defaults. Pin versions and verify correct results first. If code is reimplemented, disclose that and test its relevant behavior.</p>\n<p>Write workload conditions next to the baseline: sizes, selectivity, skew, join pattern, concurrency, reads/writes, freshness and cache state. Include the stress condition the claim concerns. A comparison only in a favorable regime cannot establish a broader benefit.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A strong baseline removes alternative explanations for a win.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-33-s2","title":"Worked example: aggregate speedup can reverse the story","html":"<p>Take two baseline times, 1 s and 100 s. Proposed times are 0.5 s and 110 s. Query speedups are 1/0.5=2 and 100/110≈0.909. Their arithmetic mean is (2+0.909)/2≈1.455; the geometric mean is √(2×0.909)≈1.348. Both exceed one.</p>\n<p>Now execute the fixed workload once: baseline=1+100=101 s; proposed=0.5+110=110.5 s. Workload speedup=101/110.5≈0.914, so the workload slows down. Saving 0.5 s cannot pay for losing 10 s. The geometric mean weights query ratios equally on a logarithmic scale; workload total weights actual time. Report which question each answers.</p>\n<details><summary>Nine queries save 1 ms each; one loses 100 ms. What is the total change?</summary><p>Saved=9×1=9 ms; added=100 ms; net=91 ms slower. A “nine of ten wins” count hides magnitude. Show regressions as well as summary ratios.</p></details>\n<p>Tail percentiles also need a declared quantile convention and enough observations. Ten values do not give stable p99 evidence; correlated queries within one run are not independent repetitions.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-33-s3","title":"Control the measurement boundary","html":"<p>Draw the request timeline: creation → preprocessing → queue → planning → transfer/execution → final response. Mark the interval timed by each metric. A GPU launch can return before device work finishes, so use suitable synchronization or device events for execution and separately measure complete wall time.</p>\n<p>Warm-up can load a model, compile code, populate caches and connect services. If the claim includes startup or elastic scaling, report cold and warm paths. Alternate or randomize system order to reduce drift; log shared-machine activity.</p>\n<p>Fix request count/duration, repetitions, warm-up, stopping and failure policy before comparing. A 30-second timeout tells us the run did not finish by 30 seconds; it is censored evidence, not a row to delete or an exact 30-second completion. Report failures and useful lower bounds without inventing finish times.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-33-s4","title":"Build a minimal evaluation matrix","html":"<p>Begin with a tiny smoke test checking installation and correct outputs. Then expand a small matrix only along axes that challenge the claim: perhaps two sizes, easy/typical/stress workloads, two resource limits and a validation-only parameter sweep. The full cross-product can be expensive without answering a better question.</p>\n<p>Choose split units from intended generalization: template for learned optimization, source document/entity for extraction, time for future workloads. ANN needs exact reference neighbors under the same metric and eligible set. A cloud prototype needs commit/recovery validation separately from timing.</p>\n<p>Keep raw runs and exclusion reasons. Environment outages and method crashes are different, but neither should vanish silently.</p>\n<details><summary>The baseline gets ten tuning trials and the proposal gets 500. How can this be made fair?</summary><p>Equalize or justify budgets on separate validation data and report search cost. If that is impossible, disclose the asymmetry and restrict the conclusion. More search alone may explain the chosen operating point.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-33-s5","title":"Report uncertainty and make the consultation useful","html":"<p>For a fixed-workload claim, one complete workload run is one experimental repetition. Compare three hypothetical matched pairs: baseline 100,104,98 ms; proposed 98,100,99 ms. Subtract within pairs: savings 2,4,−1 ms. Sum=5 ms; mean saving=5/3≈1.67 ms; range=−1 to 4 ms. One pair regresses. These three pairs are descriptive evidence, not a confidence interval or a broad guarantee.</p>\n<p>With justified additional independent pairs, estimate uncertainty for the paired effect. Resample whole pairs if resampling is appropriate; treating every correlated query as independent invents precision. Statistical significance and useful practical size are different questions.</p>\n<details><summary>A system is 2% faster and runs vary about 5%. Is there no benefit?</summary><p>Those percentages alone cannot decide. Inspect independent sample count, paired differences and a suitable interval for the effect. Improve controls or collect justified repetitions if uncertainty remains wide, then judge whether the possible benefit matters in practice.</p></details>\n<p>Bring the baseline table, timeline, one raw trace and evaluation matrix to consultation. Ask which omitted workload most threatens the claim, record actual advice, and apply protocol changes consistently.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Quality, runtime, resources and failures belong in the same evidence package.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which report best handles a timed-out query?","options":["Remove it from all summaries.","Count it as zero seconds.","Report the timeout threshold and failure count, and avoid pretending the exact runtime is known.","Replace it with the median successful runtime."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timeout gives censored information. Dropping it can systematically hide regressions.","id":"supplement-33-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["lqo","cuvsbench"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-33-s1","supplement-33-s2","supplement-33-s3","supplement-33-s4","supplement-33-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf","label":"Is Your Learned Query Optimizer Behaving As You Expect? A Machine Learning Perspective · Published peer-reviewed research / evaluation"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":12,"week":13,"syllabusTopics":["project clinic","baseline selection","measurement methodology","experimental design","uncertainty and timeouts"],"topics":["project clinic","baseline selection","measurement methodology","experimental design","uncertainty and timeouts"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["lqo","cuvsbench"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-34","lecture":null,"order":34,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M12 · Session 3","module":12,"week":14,"suggestedSession":3,"title":"Project clinic: ablations, counterexamples and negative results","short":"Mechanisms · factorial tests · robustness · claims","group":"12 · Project clinic","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"An ablation asks why the system works. A counterexample shows where the explanation stops working.","goals":["Design ablations that isolate mechanisms without quietly changing the task.","Interpret interacting components using a small factorial experiment.","Turn failures and negative results into bounded, useful conclusions."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-34-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>A new system adds caching and batching and finishes in 40 ms instead of 100 ms. Which change helped? The final comparison cannot tell us. We will switch each mechanism on and off, then test the assumptions behind the explanation.</p>\n<p>Bring the question and baseline from lessons 32–33. The four-cell numbers are original hypothetical measurements for reasoning, not an actual benchmark. This supplemental clinic is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; assessment rules, required deliverables and deadlines remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-34-s1","title":"From a system result to a mechanism claim","html":"<p>If a system changes batching, caching and candidate selection together, its final win leaves three possible causes and their interactions. To isolate one, remove or vary it while keeping the same task, quality requirement and timing protocol. This is why an ablation is more informative than another final-system chart.</p>\n<p>But removal must leave a coherent system. A cache removal that leaves invalid pointers does not measure caching's value. A fixed-parameter ablation asks about a local effect at one configuration. A retuned ablation asks how well the system can do without that component. They are different questions; report both when relevant and label them.</p>\n<p>Pick controls from the explanation. If benefits supposedly come from smarter candidates, match candidate counts. If from better memory use, match memory. For learning, consider a simple model, equal training budgets or random-label control. An obviously broken or unfairly tuned comparison cannot establish necessity.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>“Without X” is meaningful only when the remaining system still implements a coherent comparison.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-34-s2","title":"Worked example: interactions between cache and batching","html":"<p>Use the same workload and quality in all four cells. With cache off and batching off, latency t00=100 ms. Batching alone gives t01=80 ms. Cache alone gives t10=70 ms. Both give t11=40 ms.</p>\n<p>First hold batching off: cache saves 100−70=30 ms. With batching on, cache saves 80−40=40 ms. Next hold cache off: batching saves 100−80=20 ms. With cache on, batching saves 70−40=30 ms. The effect depends on the other component.</p>\n<p>If savings were additive, both would take 100−30−20=50 ms. The hypothetical result is 40 ms, an extra 10 ms saving. Define signed latency interaction I=t11−t10−t01+t00=40−70−80+100=−10 ms. Negative here means latency below the additive prediction. Repeat the four cells and inspect variability before trusting this effect.</p>\n<details><summary>A helps only when B is present. Is A useless?</summary><p>No. The evidence suggests an interaction. Trace cache hits, requests and scheduling to test a cause; arithmetic alone does not prove it. Also state the metric scale: additive milliseconds and multiplicative speedup are different analyses.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-34-s3","title":"Stress the assumptions deliberately","html":"<p>Write an assumption ledger with five columns: assumption, reason needed, deliberate violation, predicted failure, observed result. That turns a vague “robustness test” into a checkable question.</p>\n<p>For a learned estimator, hold out a template or change the data distribution. For ANN, try a selective filter, near-tied neighbors, skewed clusters, deletes or an embedding-version change. For document processing, use a long document, OCR noise, ambiguity, conflicting evidence or a model revision. For disaggregation, try cold caches, hot-page contention, network delay, partial failures and ownership churn.</p>\n<p>Choose cases relevant to the intended claim. Start with a small reproducible counterexample where the state can be inspected. The goal is to discover a boundary, not to manufacture an irrelevant corner case against another method. Keep input, expected behavior, actual trace and explanation together.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-34-s4","title":"A negative result is a result after the controls are checked","html":"<p>Suppose adaptive ANN search performs 30% fewer distance calculations but user latency does not change. First verify result quality, implementation, baseline resources, workload generator and timing boundary. Then inspect where time went rather than immediately adding another model.</p>\n<p>An original breakdown might show host orchestration and transfer dominate. Reduced device work can be real without improving that host-bound regime. The next experiment could increase batch size or remove transfers under an explicitly different boundary. Preserve the original negative result; do not rewrite it as a success.</p>\n<details><summary>Q-error improves but runtime worsens. Is the study invalid?</summary><p>Not automatically. After correctness and timing checks, test whether inference overhead, plan-threshold errors or metric misalignment explains it. A controlled mismatch is useful evidence. Record exploratory datasets instead of searching until a favorable one appears and presenting only that run; freeze a confirmatory protocol for a stronger claim.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-34-s5","title":"Revise the claim and consultation decisions","html":"<p>Sort planned claims into supported, not established and contradicted using the actual evidence. If batching helps only at high arrival rates, replace a universal speed claim with that measured range. Add the counterexample and state which assumption changes there.</p>\n<p>Bring the four-cell comparison, one failure trace, assumption ledger and proposed scope change to consultation. A decision record can contain date, evidence, decision, rationale, owner and next check; it is a suggested working aid, not an official submission form.</p>\n<details><summary>The gain appears only after removing high-contention runs. What should the report say?</summary><p>Show all runs and the contention-dependent breakdown. Restrict the benefit to the measured low-contention regime. If high contention belongs to the target workload, include its failure in the overall conclusion. Removing a run solely because it hurts the headline is not a valid exclusion.</p></details><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A credible limitation explains when and why the result changes.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"What does a four-cell experiment with A off/on and B off/on reveal that two independent ablations may miss?","options":["The official grading rubric.","A guarantee of generalization to every workload.","Interactions between A and B.","Whether all bugs are gone."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Compute each effect with the other component fixed. In the lesson cache saves 30 ms without batching and 40 ms with batching; batching saves 20 or 30 ms. The signed latency interaction is 40−70−80+100=−10 ms, an extra saving beyond the additive prediction, subject to repeated-measurement uncertainty.","id":"supplement-34-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["lqo","grasp","cuvsbench"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-34-s1","supplement-34-s2","supplement-34-s3","supplement-34-s4","supplement-34-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol17/p1565-lehmann.pdf","label":"Is Your Learned Query Optimizer Behaving As You Expect? A Machine Learning Perspective · Published peer-reviewed research / evaluation"},{"url":"https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p2519-wu.pdf","label":"Data-Agnostic Cardinality Learning from Imperfect Workloads · Published peer-reviewed research"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":12,"week":14,"syllabusTopics":["project clinic","ablation studies","mechanism analysis","negative results","research consultation"],"topics":["project clinic","ablation studies","mechanism analysis","negative results","research consultation"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["lqo","grasp","cuvsbench"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-35","lecture":null,"order":35,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M12 · Session 4","module":12,"week":14,"suggestedSession":4,"title":"Project clinic: reproducible artifacts and defensible conclusions","short":"Environment · data lineage · smoke tests · report assembly","group":"12 · Project clinic","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"A useful artifact lets another person trace a figure back to inputs, code, settings and raw measurements.","goals":["Package the evidence required to rerun a result.","Separate a smoke test from a full scientific reproduction.","Audit cost, data permission, limitations and claims before presenting."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-35-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>A teammate has the code but cannot rebuild your chart. Which missing link stops them: input, configuration, timing protocol or raw output? This lesson assembles the chain and budgets the work before a full run.</p>\n<p>Bring your frozen question, protocol and claim from lessons 32–34. All manifests and budget figures are original suggestions, not course submission requirements. This supplemental clinic is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; the instructor's real rules, deadlines and submission route remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-35-s1","title":"Define the artifact boundary","html":"<p>Start at the figure and walk backward: plotted value → raw results → command/configuration → code and dependencies → input identity → environment. A repository link covers only part of this chain. Someone following it must be able to see what produced the claimed observation.</p>\n<p>Record hardware model/count, memory, driver/runtime, interconnect, CPU/RAM/storage, operating system and concurrency limits. A container preserves much software state, but not identical hardware, external services, data or all numerical behavior.</p>\n<p>For inputs, record provenance, version, preprocessing and license. If full data cannot be shared, provide a small public or synthetic check and say which results cannot be independently rerun. Do not redistribute restricted files or credentials. Artifact-evaluation guidance offers useful distinctions between an artifact that runs and evidence that results reproduce; this companion grants no badge or course status.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Reproducibility is a traceable chain of evidence, not just a link to code.</p></aside><p class=\"source-note\">Sources for this section: <a href=\"https://sigsim.acm.org/conf/pads/2024/blog/artifact-evaluation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACM SIGSIM PADS 2024: Reproducibility and Artifact Evaluation</a>. Numerical traces marked original are teaching examples, not reported measurements.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-35-s2","title":"Worked example: a minimal run manifest","html":"<p>Construct one original run manifest as a chain of answers. Identity: experiment ID, commit, configuration hash, timestamp and responsible member. Inputs: dataset version/hash, query split, model/embedding/prompt revision and seed. Environment: package lock, runtime, hardware, resource limits and background load.</p>\n<p>Protocol: warm-up, repetitions, trial order, timing boundary, timeout and quality target. Outputs: raw-result path, error logs, correctness result, figure and generation command. Cost: CPU/device elapsed use, service calls, peak memory and any price assumptions. A checksum detects changed bytes; it does not prove correct data.</p>\n<details><summary>The README says “run latest on the standard dataset.” What prevents a rerun?</summary><p>Both names can change. Specify exact code/dependencies, data/preprocessing, command/configuration, environment and protocol, then connect raw outputs to the figure. Keep secrets out of version control and inspect logs for sensitive query or document text before sharing.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-35-s3","title":"A two-level execution path","html":"<p>Offer two execution paths. The smoke test uses tiny public/synthetic input, runs quickly and checks installation, schema, expected results and output paths. If that fails, debugging is cheap. Label it clearly: it does not reproduce paper-scale performance.</p>\n<p>The full path lists actual preparation, resource/time needs, evaluation command, raw-output format and figure command. Include restart/retry behavior and explicit expensive API or disk requirements; a setup script must not silently launch paid cloud work.</p>\n<p>Ask a teammate to follow instructions in a clean allowed environment. Record changed defaults, missing paths, unavailable dependencies and nondeterminism. If the chart changes, investigate the chain instead of copying the old chart.</p>\n<details><summary>The smoke test matches expected output. Is the benchmark reproduced?</summary><p>No. The small case validates the basic path. Performance reproduction requires the declared scale, environment, protocol and comparison, with differences reported. It is useful to know exactly which level passed.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-35-s4","title":"Budget the experiment rather than only the final run","html":"<p>Budget every repeated operation. One query repetition takes one GPU for six minutes. Twenty configurations × five repetitions × six minutes =600 GPU-minutes=10 GPU-hours. Data preparation is one CPU-hour once; keep that resource separate.</p>\n<p>If each configuration's index build takes four GPU-minutes and is reused, builds add 20×4=80 GPU-minutes. Total=680 GPU-minutes=11⅓ GPU-hours. Rebuilding before every repetition instead adds 20×5×4=400; total=1,000 GPU-minutes=16⅔ GPU-hours. Builds can be outside query timing while remaining inside the experiment budget.</p>\n<p>GPU-hours are not wall time when work overlaps or uses multiple devices. CPU-hours cannot simply be added as GPU-hours. Include failed runs, profiling, tuning and artifact checks. For one/two H100s, inspect memory headroom before allocating. Plan a small pilot and an explicit budget stop; one successful run cannot answer variability.</p>\n<details><summary>Why state whether builds are reused?</summary><p>The query comparison might be unchanged, but the total resource estimate differs by 320 GPU-minutes. A reader deciding feasibility needs the reuse rule, not just the final search time.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-35-s5","title":"Assemble a report that does not overclaim","html":"<p>Build the report around one question and the evidence answering it: motivation, related work, design/assumptions, methodology, results, ablations, limitations and artifact path. Put each claim next to its supporting figure, with units, sample count and quality target visible.</p>\n<p>Maintain a claim-evidence ledger. For “scales better,” identify the scaling axis, fixed resources, workload, uncertainty and figure. Separate measured results, theoretical guarantees, analytical estimates and future work. Hypothetical illustration is not measured evidence.</p>\n<details><summary>You computed memory from vector dimensions but did not instrument allocations. How should it appear?</summary><p>As an analytical estimate, with workspace and other omissions listed. It is not measured peak memory. If fitting memory is central to the claim, measure it before concluding feasibility.</p></details>\n<p>Make sure each member can explain the central result and one failure. Assemble actual required deliverables only from the instructor's confirmed instructions.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>State what ran, what was measured and what remains an inference.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which item most directly links a plotted point to its scientific inputs?","options":["A run manifest with code/data/configuration versions and raw result references.","The number of repository stars.","The project logo.","A screenshot without units."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A manifest supports the evidence chain from inputs and protocol to raw results and the figure.","id":"supplement-35-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-35-s1","supplement-35-s2","supplement-35-s3","supplement-35-s4","supplement-35-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://sigsim.acm.org/conf/pads/2024/blog/artifact-evaluation/","label":"ACM SIGSIM PADS 2024: Reproducibility and Artifact Evaluation · Official ACM conference guidance; used as a general example, not the course rubric"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":12,"week":14,"syllabusTopics":["project clinic","reproducibility","artifact packaging","research reporting","resource planning"],"topics":["project clinic","reproducibility","artifact packaging","research reporting","resource planning"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-36","lecture":null,"order":36,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M13 · Session 1","module":13,"week":15,"suggestedSession":1,"title":"Research symposium: build a conference-style research story","short":"Claim · mechanism · evidence · limitations · group handoffs","group":"13 · Research symposium","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"Your audience should leave knowing the problem, the idea, the evidence and the condition under which the idea stops helping.","goals":["Organize a talk around one supported claim.","Explain a mechanism with a small worked example before benchmarks.","Design readable evidence slides and coordinated group handoffs."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-36-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>“Our system is twice as fast” is memorable, but an audience cannot tell whether it helps a user. We will build a short argument from two arriving queries, then connect it to a clearly labeled hypothetical benchmark claim.</p>\n<p>Bring one supported claim, its evidence and a failure case. This supplemental symposium practice is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment. Presentation length, question period, contribution reporting, assessment and submission instructions remain unverified; use actual instructor instructions when supplied.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-36-s1","title":"The research story in four questions","html":"<p>An audience first needs the problem: what do users wait for? Then the bottleneck: why does the current path struggle? Then the mechanism: what changes operationally? Finally the evidence: what was measured under which conditions? These four questions form an argument the listener can reconstruct.</p>\n<p>For our ANN example, name the workload, resource boundary and recall requirement at the start. Reuse a few queries to show immediate dispatch and batching. Explain the added waiting cost along with reduced device work. If a listener can predict when batching loses, they understand more than a headline.</p>\n<p>Choose one central claim and supporting observations. Implementation history belongs only when it explains a decision or failure. A chronological diary of debugging makes listeners assemble the argument themselves; show them the causal path directly.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A clear talk lets the audience reconstruct the argument, not just remember the headline.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-36-s2","title":"An adaptable presentation outline","html":"<p>Start the talk with the question and scope. Show a baseline trace so the bottleneck is visible. Introduce one operational change, then state the methodology needed to test it: dataset, baseline, resources, quality target and timing boundary.</p>\n<p>Only then show the main comparison, with units and uncertainty large enough to read. Follow it with an ablation or counterexample explaining why the change helps and where it does not. End with the supported answer, a specific limitation and an artifact path.</p>\n<p>This is an adaptable sequence, not mandatory slide headings. Fit it to the actual allotted time rather than inventing course timing. Rehearse by asking whether each transition resolves a question raised on the preceding slide. If a chart needs many unexplained definitions, introduce a small worked example before it.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-36-s3","title":"Worked example: replace a headline with an argument","html":"<p>Original mechanism trace: Q1 arrives at 0 ms and Q2 at 1 ms. Immediate serial execution takes 2 ms each. Q1 runs 0–2, Q2 runs 2–4; response times are 2−0=2 and 4−1=3 ms.</p>\n<p>Batching waits until 1 ms, then processes both in an assumed 2 ms. Both finish at 3 ms. Response times are 3−0=3 and 3−1=2 ms. The pair finishes earlier, but the first query waits longer. If only Q1 arrives, the same 1 ms wait plus 2 ms execution gives 3 ms instead of 2 ms with no batching benefit. These supplied costs illustrate a mechanism; they do not derive the benchmark numbers below.</p>\n<p>A stronger hypothetical claim than “2× faster” is: on a fixed public million-vector workload, batching raises throughput from 8,000 to 16,000 queries/s at recall@10=0.95, while p95 response latency rises from 4 to 7 ms. It doubles throughput at that quality but increases latency. Name arrival pattern, hardware, timing boundary, failures and uncertainty before presenting such numbers as your own measurements.</p>\n<details><summary>Why is this stronger even though it admits a cost?</summary><p>The audience can decide whether throughput matters enough and whether 7 ms meets their latency budget. A throughput-only headline cannot answer that user question.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-36-s4","title":"Group presentation as one coherent explanation","html":"<p>Divide speaking roles by the argument rather than by source files. One person can introduce the question and baseline, another the mechanism, another methodology/results. Each still needs to know the central claim and limitation so transitions preserve meaning.</p>\n<p>Agree on terms, units, colors and notation. A useful transition carries a question: “The trace predicts fewer transfers; now we test whether transfers dominate real latency.” Practice it with the next speaker, not as an isolated sentence.</p>\n<p>Record actual contributions accurately instead of inventing equal participation or saying someone ran an experiment they did not. Keep a coordination list for order and backups. A failed live demo can be replaced by an explicitly recorded trace or screenshot; do not label a recording live. Follow confirmed course contribution requirements separately.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-36-s5","title":"Citations, backup evidence and rehearsal","html":"<p>A reused algorithm, figure or reported external result needs a visible short source and full citation/link. Label author-reported numbers separately from your experiments. Check figure/data licenses or make an original explanation of the underlying idea.</p>\n<p>Prepare backup evidence for data splits, baseline tuning, hardware, quality, failures, ablations and raw traces. Rehearse once for logic, once for actual timing and once with skeptical questions. Ask an unfamiliar listener to restate both claim and limitation; inability to do so shows where the explanation needs repair.</p>\n<details><summary>You need a paper's chart but did not reproduce it. What can you say?</summary><p>Attribute it as the authors' reported result with its conditions. Do not merge it into your measured series. Follow reuse permission or draw an original summary.</p></details>\n<details><summary>A demo fails in rehearsal. What is the useful next step?</summary><p>Diagnose and record the failure, narrow unsupported claims and prepare a clearly labeled saved trace. Hiding failure removes evidence about reliability and leaves no plan when it happens again.</p></details><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>A polished presentation makes assumptions and limits easier to see.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"Which main-result slide is most defensible?","options":["A comparison stating workload, baseline, units, quality target and uncertainty.","A speedup with no baseline name.","An unlabeled best-case screenshot.","A paper’s plot labeled as the group’s own result."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A scientific comparison needs enough context to interpret what improved and what was held fixed.","id":"supplement-36-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-36-s1","supplement-36-s2","supplement-36-s3","supplement-36-s4","supplement-36-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://sigsim.acm.org/conf/pads/2024/blog/artifact-evaluation/","label":"ACM SIGSIM PADS 2024: Reproducibility and Artifact Evaluation · Official ACM conference guidance; used as a general example, not the course rubric"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":13,"week":15,"syllabusTopics":["research symposium","conference-style group presentation","presentation structure","evidence communication","research attribution"],"topics":["research symposium","conference-style group presentation","presentation structure","evidence communication","research attribution"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench"]},"lab":null},{"id":"supplement-37","lecture":null,"order":37,"kind":"supplemental","displayLabel":"M13 · Session 2","module":13,"week":15,"suggestedSession":2,"title":"Research symposium: defend, review and close the evidence loop","short":"Questions · peer review · limits · research synthesis","group":"13 · Research symposium","time":35,"timeMeaning":"Suggested independent study minutes; not scheduled contact time","lead":"A good answer in discussion separates what the experiment establishes from what would require another experiment.","goals":["Answer critical questions with evidence and calibrated uncertainty.","Give actionable peer feedback without inventing a grading scheme.","Identify the next experiment and preserve the final artifact."],"sections":[{"id":"supplement-37-status","title":"Start here: prerequisites and course status","html":"<p>An audience asks whether a one-GPU result doubles on two GPUs. The honest answer depends on evidence already collected, not confidence in the speaker. This lesson practices answering precisely, reviewing another group's claim and preserving what the discussion changes.</p>\n<p>Bring the claim, limits and artifact path from lesson 36. Questions and review forms are original practice suggestions. This supplemental session is not an Edinburgh lecture or verified Canvas assignment; grading weights, deadlines, upload destinations and mandatory formats remain unverified.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-37-s1","title":"Prepare for four kinds of questions","html":"<p>Listen to the full question, then identify what it challenges. A meaning question asks the scope: workload, metric and operating condition. A fairness question asks whether quality, resources and tuning were comparable. A mechanism question asks what ablation supports the explanation. A generality question asks about a different scale, skew, concurrency, drift or hardware.</p>\n<p>Answer the narrow point first and point to the relevant protocol or trace. Do not substitute a favorable number from another condition. If a question exposes a flaw, name its effect on the conclusion. If evidence is missing, say what is unmeasured and which experiment would resolve it.</p>\n<p>For a theory claim, this may mean restating assumptions and a counterexample outside them. For a system claim, it may mean showing the timing boundary or failure count. Both are stronger than improvising a result that was never run.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>“We have not measured that” is a useful scientific answer when followed by the experiment that would resolve it.</p></aside>"},{"id":"supplement-37-s2","title":"Worked discussion: three strong responses","html":"<p>Question: “Would two H100s double throughput?” If only one was measured, say that. Routing, placement and communication remain unmeasured. If both were measured, give the actual ratio under matched workload and quality with overheads; device count does not derive speedup.</p>\n<p>Question: “The median improves; what about slow requests?” If tails/failures were collected, show p95/p99 and timeout breakdown, including regressing subgroups. If not, the median cannot supply their behavior: state that limitation and propose a timeout-aware tail test.</p>\n<p>Question: “Does your LLM evaluator prove correctness?” Agreement alone measures agreement. Explain the independent held-out human/factual check only if it actually happened, with its guide and sample. Shared evaluator errors remain possible.</p>\n<details><summary>Why is “not measured yet” an incomplete but useful answer?</summary><p>It protects the boundary of the current evidence. Complete it with a test: match recall and workload, run one/two devices, include routing and merge time, and report failures. Now the uncertainty has an actionable resolution.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-37-s3","title":"Peer review that helps another group improve","html":"<p>Begin peer review by restating the group's central claim in your own words. Record its strongest evidence, one unclear assumption, an alternative explanation and one discriminating test or presentation change. Add a course connection that helps them reason, not just a score.</p>\n<p>For example, replace “bad ANN benchmark” with “the two QPS points have different recall, so this graph does not isolate speed at equal quality.” Ask whether matched-quality sweeps exist. That is a testable methodological concern. If the sweep exists but the slide omits it, the issue is missing explanation instead.</p>\n<p>Theory review asks assumptions, statement, proof idea and counterexamples. Systems review asks correctness, resources, workload and comparison. Neither is assessed solely by a speed chart. This discussion form is suggested practice, not an official rubric.</p>\n<details><summary>A carefully controlled study reports no gain. What useful feedback remains?</summary><p>Check whether the hypothesis was meaningful and tests ruled out plausible benefits under the stated conditions. Suggest the experiment most likely to distinguish the remaining explanations. Do not require a positive speedup to recognize a supported finding.</p></details>"},{"id":"supplement-37-s4","title":"Synthesize the frontier themes with the core course","html":"<p>Connect the frontier topics to familiar responsibilities. Learned cardinalities change selectivity and plan decisions. Vector search changes how an index trades work for approximation. Semantic pipelines revisit operator order but must define permitted label error. Disaggregation moves buffers, logging and ownership across layers.</p>\n<p>Ask the same question in each: which contract is preserved while cost falls? Exact relational operators may require equivalent SQL results. ANN needs a quality target within an eligible set. Semantic operators need a reference or labeling policy. Recovery needs durability and permitted histories.</p>\n<p>Then locate the optimized layer and the claimed benefit. A faster kernel can leave application latency unchanged. Better average estimates can still produce harmful threshold decisions. Faster steady-state access can coexist with worse restart behavior. Use one trace from your project to explain that gap rather than claiming every layer inherits the improvement.</p>"},{"id":"supplement-37-s5","title":"Close the project with an evidence handoff","html":"<p>After discussion, preserve final code/configuration, input instructions, raw outputs, report/slides and an errata or open-question note. Record factual corrections and revise unsupported wording. Follow the instructor's actual submission and sharing process; this companion specifies no deadline, upload location or marking weight.</p>\n<p>Choose the next test by how much it could change your belief. A small counterexample separating queueing from device work may be more informative than a larger version of the same benchmark. State which explanations it distinguishes.</p>\n<details><summary>A figure labeled milliseconds as seconds. What must be corrected?</summary><p>Check the raw data, fix the axis and every derived claim, preserve an explanation of the correction and inform the relevant audience through the actual course process. Check neighboring figures too; do not assume the error is isolated.</p></details>\n<p>The final handoff should let another reader see what is supported, what changed during discussion and exactly how to run the next check.</p><aside class=\"important-note\" role=\"note\" aria-label=\"Key takeaway\"><strong>Key takeaway</strong><p>Finish with a trustworthy answer and a reproducible path to the next question.</p></aside>"}],"quiz":{"question":"A question asks about a hardware configuration you never tested. What is the best response?","options":["Claim linear scaling because the algorithm is parallel.","Invent a plausible number.","State the measurement limit and describe the needed scaling experiment.","Change the subject to the demo."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Calibrated uncertainty preserves the boundary between evidence and inference.","id":"supplement-37-q1","origin":"Original supplemental exercise; not a verified assessed question","sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench","lp"],"lessonSectionIds":["supplement-37-s1","supplement-37-s2","supplement-37-s3","supplement-37-s4","supplement-37-s5"]},"source":{"summary":"Original supplemental explanation and exercises. Primary research/software evidence is linked; syllabus alignment is based on the user-provided outline. Canvas-assigned readings are unverified.","links":[{"url":"https://sigsim.acm.org/conf/pads/2024/blog/artifact-evaluation/","label":"ACM SIGSIM PADS 2024: Reproducibility and Artifact Evaluation · Official ACM conference guidance; used as a general example, not the course rubric"},{"url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuvs/user-guide/benchmarking-guide/methodologies","label":"NVIDIA cuVS: Benchmarking methodologies · Official software documentation, checked 2026-10-11"},{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14075","label":"Join Size Bounds using Lp-Norms on Degree Sequences · Accepted PODS 2024 paper; linked arXiv is an author manuscript first posted in 2023"}]},"coverage":{"verified":false,"status":"Supplemental content with verified primary references; official Canvas reading alignment unverified","sourceEvidenceVerified":true,"officialCourseMaterialVerified":false,"module":13,"week":15,"syllabusTopics":["research symposium","group presentations","research defense","peer feedback","research synthesis"],"topics":["research symposium","group presentations","research defense","peer feedback","research synthesis"],"limitations":["Canvas-assigned paper list is unavailable.","Lesson is an original supplement, not a reconstructed official lecture.","No assessment rubric, deadline, presentation duration or submission format is asserted."],"sourceKeys":["artifact","cuvsbench","lp"]},"lab":null}];
